decisions.", "estimatedTime": "2 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may ignore which issues are blockers, which are manageable, and which owner must resolve them before the next engineering step.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "risk category, consequence, severity, escalation owner, recommended next action. Required workbook field focus: Risk ID; risk category; observed evidence; consequence; severity; owner; escalation reason; recommended next action.", "reason_behind_concept": "Risk classification makes blockers and escalation ownership visible before the design, simulation, integration, timing, or validation path proceeds. You identify reset and CDC ownership gaps and escalates them to the senior FPGA or architecture owner instead of attempting to solve ownership or clock-domain design decisions.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L1 FPGA design evidence readiness before HDL implementation review course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: Risk and Escalation Note. Module problem context: You identify risks: reset ambiguity may cause incorrect initialization, width mismatch may break interface behaviour, missing valid ranges may lead to incorrect validation, clock-domain ownership gaps may hide CDC issues, and incomplete memory mapping may cause software-access errors. You must classify risk severity and route each issue to the correct owner. You identify reset and CDC ownership gaps and escalates them to the senior FPGA or architecture owner instead of attempting to solve ownership or clock-domain design decisions."}, "student_confusion": "Which observations are minor, which are risks, and which are blockers that need owner confirmation?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L1 Exceptions, Blockers, Risks & Escalations", "module_specific_problem_statement": "You identify risks: reset ambiguity may cause incorrect initialization, width mismatch may break interface behaviour, missing valid ranges may lead to incorrect validation, clock-domain ownership gaps may hide CDC issues, and incomplete memory mapping may cause software-access errors. You must classify risk severity and route each issue to the correct owner. You identify reset and CDC ownership gaps and escalates them to the senior FPGA or architecture owner instead of attempting to solve ownership or clock-domain design decisions."}, {"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l1-m5", "title": "Quality, Communication & Closure", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L1_M5_Quality_Closure_Template; you evidence inputs: 12-signal interface list; top-level HDL skeleton; draft testbench; memory-map note; clock/reset description; design comment log; workbook field focus: Work completed; evidence used; checks passed; checks failed; open issues; closure message; confidence level; closure decision.", "tool_type_free_or_paid": "template + evidence pack"}, "stages": [{"key": "engage", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explore", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explain", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "express", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "empower", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "evolve", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}], "status": "available", "content": [{"id": "c-emb-fpga-l1-m5-empower", "url": "https://storage-sp.rareminds.in/LTE/embedd/mod5_01_artifact.pptx", "order": 5, "stage": "empower", "title": "Decision check for Quality and Closure Report: defend L1 readiness using visible evidence and limitations", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for Quality and Closure Report: What decision can you defend from the evidence at this stage?", "follow_up": "Make a defensible closure decision without overclaiming design correctness."}], "content_type": "presentation", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"