 "description": "Build the Final Portfolio Pack by completing this L1 Embedded Design Engineer task without truncating the case context: The reviewer must see a connected evidence chain, not disconnected notes. You must assemble readiness, intake, validation, action, risk, and closure artifacts into a portfolio that proves L1 FPGA design-readiness capability. The final portfolio must show how you moved from evidence-readiness understanding to intake, validation, handoff, escalation, closure, and portfolio proof without exceeding L1 responsibility.", "estimatedTime": "4 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may submit separate worksheets without proving a connected artifact chain, decision trail, and transfer readiness for the next level.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "artifact chain, evidence map, retry log, capability proof, transfer note, reviewer validation. Required workbook field focus: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation.", "reason_behind_concept": "Portfolio validation proves capability maturity by connecting every artifact into one evidence-backed decision trail. The final portfolio must show how you moved from evidence-readiness understanding to intake, validation, handoff, escalation, closure, and portfolio proof without exceeding L1 responsibility.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L1 FPGA design evidence readiness before HDL implementation review course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: Final Portfolio Pack. Module problem context: The reviewer must see a connected evidence chain, not disconnected notes. You must assemble readiness, intake, validation, action, risk, and closure artifacts into a portfolio that proves L1 FPGA design-readiness capability. The final portfolio must show how you moved from evidence-readiness understanding to intake, validation, handoff, escalation, closure, and portfolio proof without exceeding L1 responsibility."}, "student_confusion": "How do I prove that my final decision came from a connected evidence trail rather than isolated tasks?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L1 Portfolio, Transfer & Final Validation", "module_specific_problem_statement": "The reviewer must see a connected evidence chain, not disconnected notes. You must assemble readiness, intake, validation, action, risk, and closure artifacts into a portfolio that proves L1 FPGA design-readiness capability. The final portfolio must show how you moved from evidence-readiness understanding to intake, validation, handoff, escalation, closure, and portfolio proof without exceeding L1 responsibility."}], "duration": "14 hours", "course_id": "crs-emb-fpga-l1", "xp_reward": 650, "started_at": null, "why_needed": "Embedded design engineers need FPGA design evidence readiness before HDL implementation review because workplace review decisions depend on visible evidence, not assumptions or unverified implementation confidence. This is an L1 evidence-readiness course: you validate whether the FPGA design evidence is sufficient for review, prepares a readiness or escalation handoff, and does not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness.", "course_code": "EMB-FPGA-L1", "description": "You can inspect a beginner FPGA design evidence pack, identify missing interface/design evidence, clarify role boundaries, and decide whether the design evidence is ready, partially ready, or blocked for basic implementation review. This is an L1 evidence-readiness course: you validate whether the FPGA design evidence is sufficient for review, prepares a readiness or escalation handoff, and does not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness.", "domain_name": "Embedded Systems / FPGA Design", "level_label": "L1", "sequence_no": 1, "completed_at": null, "how_to_build": "Learners build capability by completing seven connected artifacts using the full case evidence, required workbook fields, role boundaries, risk/escalation language, and human-first engineering judgement. This is an L1 evidence-readiness course: you validate whether the FPGA design evidence is sufficient for review, prepares a readiness or escalation handoff, and does not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness.", "current_level": 1, "priority_band": "A", "thumbnail_url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1518770660439-4636190af475?q=80&w=2670&auto=format&fit=crop", "required_level": 3, "capability_name": "Embedded FPGA Evidence Readiness", "difficulty_level": "beginner", "job_pathway_role": "Embedded Design Engineer", "problem_artifacts": [{"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l1-m0", "no": 1, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "Course Readiness Sheet", "module": "Industry, Role & Course Readiness", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Can you explain the L1 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case? Input: use the full L1 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: FPGA design-readiness pack. Evidence given to you: 12-signal interface list; top-level HDL skeleton; draft testbench; memory-map note; clock/reset description; design comment log. Evidence issues / actuals: 3 unclear reset behaviours; 2 signal-width mismatches; 4 fields without valid ranges; 5 missing testbench cases; 2 clock-domain ownership gaps; 1 incomplete memory-map section. You decision required: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Action: complete the Course Readiness Sheet by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision. Evidence to cite: L1 readiness inputs, 12-signal interface list, HDL skeleton, draft testbench, memory-map note, clock/reset description, design comment log, you role boundary, non-owned dependencies, and readiness decision meanings. Output decision: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you understands the work context before acting and can separate their own role from other engineering owners. Restriction: stay inside L1 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: Make the decision meanings visible: Ready means evidence is sufficient for basic review with no blocking gaps; Partially ready means review can continue only with listed assumptions or clarifications; Blocked means reset, signal-width, valid-range, CDC ownership, or memory-map gaps prevent safe review. Decision meanings: Ready means evidence is sufficient for basic review with no blocking gaps; Partially ready means review can continue only with listed assumptions or clarifications; Blocked means reset, signal-width, valid-range, CDC ownership, or memory-map gaps prevent safe review. You do not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l1-m1", "no": 2, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "Case Intake Note", "module": "Case Intake & Role Boundary", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Has you converted the senior engineer request into a scoped case intake by separating facts, assumptions, unknowns, role ownership, and clarification questions? Input: use the full L1 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: FPGA design-readiness pack. Evidence given to you: 12-signal interface list; top-level HDL skeleton; draft testbench; memory-map note; clock/reset description; design comment log. Evidence issues / actuals: 3 unclear reset behaviours; 2 signal-width mismatches; 4 fields without valid ranges; 5 missing testbench cases; 2 clock-domain ownership gaps; 1 incomplete memory-map section. You decision required: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Action: complete the Case Intake Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Case request; available inputs; missing information; knowns; unknowns; assumptions; role boundary; clarification questions; intake decision. Evidence to cite: senior engineer request, available design files and notes, missing reset/width/range information, clock or CDC ownership questions, memory-map uncertainty, knowns, unknowns, assumptions, and clarification questions. Output decision: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you converted a vague workplace request into a scoped technical case with clear assumptions and questions. Restriction: stay inside L1 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: Keep the case-intake boundary explicit: facts, assumptions, unknowns, owner questions, and escalation points must be separated before any HDL review action is recommended. Decision meanings: Ready means evidence is sufficient for basic review with no blocking gaps; Partially ready means review can continue only with listed assumptions or clarifications; Blocked means reset, signal-width, valid-range, CDC ownership, or memory-map gaps prevent safe review. You do not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l1-m2", "no": 3, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "Evidence Validation Checklist", "module": "Evidence / Requirement Validation", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Has you validated the actual engineering evidence for FPGA design evidence readiness before HDL implementation review using the required fields: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision.? Input: use the full L1 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: FPGA design-readiness pack. Evidence given to you: 12-signal interface list; top-level HDL skeleton; draft testbench; memory-map note; clock/reset description; design comment log. Evidence issues / actuals: 3 unclear reset behaviours; 2 signal-width mismatches; 4 fields without valid ranges; 5 missing testbench cases; 2 clock-domain ownership gaps; 1 incomplete memory-map section. You decision required: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Action: complete the Evidence Validation Checklist by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision. Evidence to cite: 3 unclear reset behaviours, 2 signal-width mismatches, 4 fields without valid ranges, 5 missing testbench stimulus cases, 2 clock-domain ownership gaps, and 1 incomplete memory-map section. Output decision: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you inspected actual evidence gaps and made a defensible proceed, retry, or block decision. Restriction: stay inside L1 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: Clarify that unclear reset behaviour, CDC ownership notes, valid-range gaps, width mismatches, missing testbench cases, and memory-map gaps are evidence-readiness findings, not final design-correctness findings. Decision meanings: Ready means evidence is sufficient for basic review with no blocking gaps; Partially ready means review can continue only with listed assumptions or clarifications; Blocked means reset, signal-width, valid-range, CDC ownership, or memory-map gaps prevent safe review. You do not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l1-m3", "no": 4, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "FPGA Design Readiness Action Note", "module": "Guided Work Action or Handoff", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Is your action or handoff note based on visible engineering evidence, documented reasoning, and a safe L1 boundary rather than unsupported fixing or overclaiming? Input: use the full L1 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: FPGA design-readiness pack. Evidence given to you: 12-signal interface list; top-level HDL skeleton; draft testbench; memory-map note; clock/reset description; design comment log. Evidence issues / actuals: 3 unclear reset behaviours; 2 signal-width mismatches; 4 fields without valid ranges; 5 missing testbench cases; 2 clock-domain ownership gaps; 1 incomplete memory-map section. You decision required: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Action: complete the FPGA Design Readiness Action Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Evidence reviewed; action performed; issue counts or observations; human-first reasoning; engineering impact; handoff message; final action decision. Evidence to cite: validated evidence gaps from the checklist, owner or dependency for each gap, readiness impact, assumptions, and handoff message; do not cite corrected HDL because you do not rewrite HDL. Output decision: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you performed a concrete engineering action and made the output usable by the next stakeholder. Restriction: stay inside L1 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: You marks evidence gaps, documents assumptions, and prepares a readiness handoff. You do not rewrite HDL, fix reset architecture, solve CDC design, or decide final implementation correctness. Decision meanings: Ready means evidence is sufficient for basic review with no blocking gaps; Partially ready means review can continue only with listed assumptions or clarifications; Blocked means reset, signal-width, valid-range, CDC ownership, or memory-map gaps prevent safe review. You do not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l1-m4", "no": 5, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "Risk and Escalation Note", "module": "Exceptions, Blockers, Risks & Escalations", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Has you identified blockers, risks, ownership gaps, severity, escalation reason, and next action using module evidence? Input: use the full L1 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: FPGA design-readiness pack. Evidence given to you: 12-signal interface list; top-level HDL skeleton; draft testbench; memory-map note; clock/reset description; design comment log. Evidence issues / actuals: 3 unclear reset behaviours; 2 signal-width mismatches; 4 fields without valid ranges; 5 missing testbench cases; 2 clock-domain ownership gaps; 1 incomplete memory-map section. You decision required: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Action: complete the Risk and Escalation Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Risk ID; risk category; observed evidence; consequence; severity; owner; escalation reason; recommended next action. Evidence to cite: reset ambiguity, CDC ownership gaps, signal-width mismatch, valid-range gaps, missing testbench cases, memory-map incompleteness, severity, owner, and escalation reason. Output decision: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you links evidence to consequence, severity, owner, and escalation logic. Restriction: stay inside L1 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: You identify reset and CDC ownership gaps and escalates them to the senior FPGA or architecture owner instead of attempting to solve ownership or clock-domain design decisions. Decision meanings: Ready means evidence is sufficient for basic review with no blocking gaps; Partially ready means review can continue only with listed assumptions or clarifications; Blocked means reset, signal-width, valid-range, CDC ownership, or memory-map gaps prevent safe review. You do not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l1-m5", "no": 6, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "Quality and Closure Report", "module": "Quality, Communication & Closure", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Is the final closure message clear about what was checked, what remains limited, what decision is defensible, and what the next team should do? Input: use the full L1 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: FPGA design-readiness pack. Evidence given to you: 12-signal interface list; top-level HDL skeleton; draft testbench; memory-map note; clock/reset description; design comment log. Evidence issues / actuals: 3 unclear reset behaviours; 2 signal-width mismatches; 4 fields without valid ranges; 5 missing testbench cases; 2 clock-domain ownership gaps; 1 incomplete memory-map section. You decision required: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Action: complete the Quality and Closure Report by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Work completed; evidence used; checks passed; checks failed; open issues; closure message; confidence level; closure decision. Evidence to cite: work completed, evidence used, checks passed or failed, open issues, owner questions, limitation language, and ready / partially ready / blocked closure decision. Output decision: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you communicate a clear, honest closure decision without overclaiming readiness. Restriction: stay inside L1 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: The closure must state the readiness decision clearly and explain whether the evidence is ready, partially ready with assumptions, or blocked by reset, signal-width, valid-range, CDC ownership, or memory-map gaps. Decision meanings: Ready means evidence is sufficient for basic review with no blocking gaps; Partially ready means review can continue only with listed assumptions or clarifications; Blocked means reset, signal-width, valid-range, CDC ownership, or memory-map gaps prevent safe review. You do not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l1-m6", "no": 7, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "Final Portfolio Pack", "module": "Portfolio, Transfer & Final Validation", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Can a reviewer trace your final decision through all prior artifacts and see a connected evidence-based capability proof for FPGA design evidence readiness before HDL implementation review? Input: use the full L1 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: FPGA design-readiness pack. Evidence given to you: 12-signal interface list; top-level HDL skeleton; draft testbench; memory-map note; clock/reset description; design comment log. Evidence issues / actuals: 3 unclear reset behaviours; 2 signal-width mismatches; 4 fields without valid ranges; 5 missing testbench cases; 2 clock-domain ownership gaps; 1 incomplete memory-map section. You decision required: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Action: complete the Final Portfolio Pack by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation. Evidence to cite: complete artifact chain from readiness, intake, validation, handoff, risk, closure, and final portfolio proof. Output decision: Ready / partially ready / blocked for basic HDL implementation review. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you connects all artifacts into a coherent capability proof. Restriction: stay inside L1 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: The final portfolio must show how you moved from evidence-readiness understanding to intake, validation, handoff, escalation, closure, and portfolio proof without exceeding L1 responsibility. Decision meanings: Ready means evidence is sufficient for basic review with no blocking gaps; Partially ready means review can continue only with listed assumptions or clarifications; Blocked means reset, signal-width, valid-range, CDC ownership, or memory-map gaps prevent safe review. You do not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness.", "status": "not_started"}], "problem_statement": {"title": "FPGA Design Evidence Readiness for Embedded Systems", "description": "An embedded systems team has received a beginner FPGA design task for an aerospace electronics unit and shared a small evidence pack before implementation review begins. The pack contains a 12-signal interface list, a top-level HDL skeleton, a draft testbench, a memory-map note, a clock/reset description, and a short design review comment log. During the first check, the evidence shows 3 unclear reset behaviours, 2 signal-width mismatches, 4 interface fields without valid ranges, 5 missing testbench stimulus cases, 2 clock-domain notes without ownership, and 1 incomplete memory-map section for a control register. You must decide whether the FPGA design evidence is ready, partially ready, or blocked for basic HDL implementation review, and submit progressive artifacts that prove role understanding, case intake, evidence validation, design-readiness action, risk escalation, closure communication, and final portfolio readiness. This is an L1 evidence-readiness course: you validate whether the FPGA design evidence is sufficient for review, prepares a readiness or escalation handoff, and does not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness."}, "certification_value": "Portfolio-ready L1 evidence for FPGA design evidence readiness before HDL implementation review.", "completion_percentage": 14, "common_course_problem_statement": "An embedded systems team has received a beginner FPGA design task for an aerospace electronics unit and shared a small evidence pack before implementation review begins. The pack contains a 12-signal interface list, a top-level HDL skeleton, a draft testbench, a memory-map note, a clock/reset description, and a short design review comment log. During the first check, the evidence shows 3 unclear reset behaviours, 2 signal-width mismatches, 4 interface fields without valid ranges, 5 missing testbench stimulus cases, 2 clock-domain notes without ownership, and 1 incomplete memory-map section for a control register. You must decide whether the FPGA design evidence is ready, partially ready, or blocked for basic HDL implementation review, and submit progressive artifacts that prove role understanding, case intake, evidence validation, design-readiness action, risk escalation, closure communication, and final portfolio readiness. This is an L1 evidence-readiness course: you validate whether the FPGA design evidence is sufficient for review, prepares a readiness or escalation handoff, and does not rewrite HDL or decide final implementation correctness."}, {"id": "lpc-emb-fpga-l2", "gap": 2, "badge": "HDL Simulation Validation", "title": "HDL Testbench and Simulation Validation for FPGA Design", "status": "not_started", "modules": [{"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l2-m0", "title": "Industry, Role & Course Readiness", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L2_M0_Simulation_Readiness_Template; you evidence inputs: Verilog/VHDL module draft; testbench with 120 stimulus cycles; waveform screenshots; simulation log; assertion summary; workbook field focus: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision.", "tool_type_free_or_paid": "template + evidence pack"}, "stages": [{"key": "engage", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explore", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explain", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "express", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "empower", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "evolve", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}], "status": "available", "content": [{"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-empower", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-empower", "order": 5, "stage": "empower", "title": "Decision check for L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet: defend L2 readiness using visible evidence and limitations", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet: What decision can you defend from the evidence at this stage?", "follow_up": "Make the decision yourself using evidence: simulation evidence acceptable, needs testbench correction, or blocked for design review; do not ask AI to decide for you."}], "content_type": "checklist", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-engage", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-engage", "order": 1, "stage": "engage", "title": "L2 L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet kickoff: workplace risk, role boundary, and why HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review cannot be assumed", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet: What workplace risk appears in this module before you propose any action?", "follow_up": "Name the workplace risk in M0 using simulation-validation evidence review, not final RTL correctness language before suggesting any action."}], "content_type": "case brief", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-evolve", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-evolve", "order": 6, "stage": "evolve", "title": "Reflection for L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet: transfer this reasoning to the next embedded FPGA level or workplace case", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet: How does this module prepare you for the next module, next level, or another embedded FPGA case?", "follow_up": "State one reusable rule for moving from this level toward L3 integration readiness without overclaiming responsibility."}], "content_type": "reflection prompt", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-explain", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-explain", "order": 3, "stage": "explain", "title": "Guide for L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet: concepts, ownership boundary, and safe you decision language", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet: Which concept or boundary explains why this evidence matters?", "follow_up": "Explain the key concept in simple engineering language and connect it to simulation-validation evidence review, not final RTL correctness."}], "content_type": "explainer", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-explore", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-explore", "order": 2, "stage": "explore", "title": "Inspect evidence for L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet: 8 failing assertions; 14 waveform mismatches; 3 untested reset scenarios; 2 missing boundary-value tests; 1 valid_signal timing mismatch", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet: What exact evidence should you inspect before filling this artifact?", "follow_up": "Point to the exact evidence for M0: HDL module draft, testbench, 120 stimulus cycles, waveform screenshots, simulation log, assertion summary, 8 failing assertions, 14 waveform mismatches, 3 untested reset scenarios, 2 missing boundary-value tests, and valid_signal timing mismatch."}], "content_type": "practice activity", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-express", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m0-express", "order": 4, "stage": "express", "title": "Template task: complete the L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet using the full module problem and required workbook fields", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet: What will you write in the artifact so a reviewer can verify your work?", "follow_up": "Write the artifact using M0 evidence; include facts, assumptions, owner boundary, and decision evidence."}], "content_type": "worksheet", "duration_seconds": 1200}], "support": {"ai_support_tips": "Ask AI to explain the L2 boundary in simple terms, then rewrite the role responsibility in your own words before filling the readiness sheet. Make the L2 boundary clear: simulation evidence can support review, but it does not prove final RTL correctness or implementation readiness by itself.", "module_continuity_link": "Creates the readiness context that feeds Module 1 case intake. Make the L2 boundary clear: simulation evidence can support review, but it does not prove final RTL correctness or implementation readiness by itself."}, "artifacts": {"empower": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l2-m0-f", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l2-m0-f1", "order": 1, "title": "L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet", "description": "Can you explain the L2 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case?", "instructions": "Complete the final artifact by answering the workplace artifact question: Can you explain the L2 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case? Fill the required workbook fields fully: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision. Reviewer expectation: Checks whether you understands the work context before acting and can separate their own role from other engineering owners. Submit with visible evidence, reasoning clarity, correct L2 role boundary, escalation where needed, limitation language, and artifact completeness. Do not use AI to produce the final answer; use AI only as a coach for guided questions and clarity checks. Level-specific review correction: Make the L2 boundary clear: simulation evidence can support review, but it does not prove final RTL correctness or implementation readiness by itself.", "accepted_file_types": [".xlsx", ".docx", ".pdf", ".zip"], "submission_required": true, "expected_submission_type": "file"}], "templates": [{"id": "at-emb-fpga-l2-m0", "version": 1, "file_url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/templates/emb-fpga-l2/ART_L2_M0_Simulation_Readiness_Template", "file_name": "ART_L2_M0_Simulation_Readiness_Template", "file_type": "xlsx", "artifact_id": "ma-emb-fpga-l2-m0-f", "question_id": "aq-emb-fpga-l2-m0-f1", "is_downloadable": true}], "submissions": [], "total_score": 100, "max_attempts": 3, "artifact_type": "final", "passing_score": 70, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": true}, "express": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l2-m0-p", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l2-m0-p1", "order": 1, "title": "Practice Draft: L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet", "description": "Can you explain the L2 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case?", "instructions": "Draft a first pass using the full module problem and required fields: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision.. Think through the evidence manually first and use AI only as a coach for questions, clarity checks, missed-risk prompts, or limitation checks. Level-specific review correction: Make the L2 boundary clear: simulation evidence can support review, but it does not prove final RTL correctness or implementation readiness by itself.", "submission_required": false, "expected_submission_type": "text"}], "templates": [], "submissions": [], "total_score": 0, "max_attempts": null, "artifact_type": "practice", "passing_score": null, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": false}}, "knowledge": {"technical_concepts_learnt": "HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review technical focus: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision. Make the L2 boundary clear: simulation evidence can support review, but it does not prove final RTL correctness or implementation readiness by itself.", "academic_refresher_concepts": "Embedded systems workflow, FPGA/HDL review basics, role responsibility, evidence readiness"}, "module_no": 0, "description": "Build the L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet by completing this L2 Embedded Design Engineer task without truncating the case context: You are moving from design-readiness to simulation validation. The risk is that a testbench may look complete because it runs, while assertions, waveform checks, reset cases, and boundary cases remain weak. You must understand what simulation evidence is needed before the design can be reviewed confidently. Make the L2 boundary clear: simulation evidence can support review, but it does not prove final RTL correctness or implementation readiness by itself.", "estimatedTime": "2 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may start technical work without understanding why HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review depends on evidence readiness, role scope, and ownership boundaries.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "work context, role boundary, evidence readiness, non-owned dependencies, readiness decision. Required workbook field focus: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision.", "reason_behind_concept": "Readiness understanding prevents you from treating a limited evidence pack as a complete engineering approval. Make the L2 boundary clear: simulation evidence can support review, but it does not prove final RTL correctness or implementation readiness by itself.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L2 HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet. Module problem context: You are moving from design-readiness to simulation validation. The risk is that a testbench may look complete because it runs, while assertions, waveform checks, reset cases, and boundary cases remain weak. You must understand what simulation evidence is needed before the design can be reviewed confidently. Make the L2 boundary clear: simulation evidence can support review, but it does not prove final RTL correctness or implementation readiness by itself."}, "student_confusion": "What does L2 mean here, and am I supposed to solve the full embedded design problem or validate readiness evidence?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L2 Industry, Role & Course Readiness", "module_specific_problem_statement": "You are moving from design-readiness to simulation validation. The risk is that a testbench may look complete because it runs, while assertions, waveform checks, reset cases, and boundary cases remain weak. You must understand what simulation evidence is needed before the design can be reviewed confidently. Make the L2 boundary clear: simulation evidence can support review, but it does not prove final RTL correctness or implementation readiness by itself."}, {"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l2-m1", "title": "Case Intake & Role Boundary", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L2_M1_Simulation_Case_Intake_Template; you evidence inputs: Verilog/VHDL module draft; testbench with 120 stimulus cycles; waveform screenshots; simulation log; assertion summary; workbook field focus: Case request; available inputs; missing information; knowns; unknowns; assumptions; role boundary; clarification questions; intake decision.", "tool_type_free_or_paid": "template + evidence pack"}, "stages": [{"key": "engage", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explore", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explain", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "express", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "empower", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "evolve", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}], "status": "available", "content": [{"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-empower", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-empower", "order": 5, "stage": "empower", "title": "Decision check for L2 Simulation Case Intake Note: defend L2 readiness using visible evidence and limitations", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Case Intake Note: What decision can you defend from the evidence at this stage?", "follow_up": "Make the decision yourself using evidence: simulation evidence acceptable, needs testbench correction, or blocked for design review; do not ask AI to decide for you."}], "content_type": "checklist", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-engage", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-engage", "order": 1, "stage": "engage", "title": "L2 L2 Simulation Case Intake Note kickoff: workplace risk, role boundary, and why HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review cannot be assumed", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Case Intake Note: What workplace risk appears in this module before you propose any action?", "follow_up": "Name the workplace risk in M1 using simulation-validation evidence review, not final RTL correctness language before suggesting any action."}], "content_type": "case brief", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-evolve", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-evolve", "order": 6, "stage": "evolve", "title": "Reflection for L2 Simulation Case Intake Note: transfer this reasoning to the next embedded FPGA level or workplace case", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Case Intake Note: How does this module prepare you for the next module, next level, or another embedded FPGA case?", "follow_up": "State one reusable rule for moving from this level toward L3 integration readiness without overclaiming responsibility."}], "content_type": "reflection prompt", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-explain", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-explain", "order": 3, "stage": "explain", "title": "Guide for L2 Simulation Case Intake Note: concepts, ownership boundary, and safe you decision language", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Case Intake Note: Which concept or boundary explains why this evidence matters?", "follow_up": "Explain the key concept in simple engineering language and connect it to simulation-validation evidence review, not final RTL correctness."}], "content_type": "explainer", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-explore", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-explore", "order": 2, "stage": "explore", "title": "Inspect evidence for L2 Simulation Case Intake Note: 8 failing assertions; 14 waveform mismatches; 3 untested reset scenarios; 2 missing boundary-value tests; 1 valid_signal timing mismatch", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Case Intake Note: What exact evidence should you inspect before filling this artifact?", "follow_up": "Point to the exact evidence for M1: HDL module draft, testbench, 120 stimulus cycles, waveform screenshots, simulation log, assertion summary, 8 failing assertions, 14 waveform mismatches, 3 untested reset scenarios, 2 missing boundary-value tests, and valid_signal timing mismatch."}], "content_type": "practice activity", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-express", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m1-express", "order": 4, "stage": "express", "title": "Template task: complete the L2 Simulation Case Intake Note using the full module problem and required workbook fields", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Case Intake Note: What will you write in the artifact so a reviewer can verify your work?", "follow_up": "Write the artifact using M1 evidence; include facts, assumptions, owner boundary, and decision evidence."}], "content_type": "worksheet", "duration_seconds": 1200}], "support": {"ai_support_tips": "Ask AI to help list clarification questions and owner boundaries; do not ask it to decide the case for you. Clarify HDL wording: the evidence pack may use either Verilog or VHDL depending on the provided module draft; you are not required to switch between both languages.", "module_continuity_link": "Uses the L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet as input and produces the L2 Simulation Case Intake Note that feeds the L2 Simulation Evidence Validation Checklist. Clarify HDL wording: the evidence pack may use either Verilog or VHDL depending on the provided module draft; you are not required to switch between both languages."}, "artifacts": {"empower": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l2-m1-f", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l2-m1-f1", "order": 1, "title": "L2 Simulation Case Intake Note", "description": "Has you converted the senior engineer request into a scoped case intake by separating facts, assumptions, unknowns, role ownership, and clarification questions?", "instructions": "Complete the final artifact by answering the workplace artifact question: Has you converted the senior engineer request into a scoped case intake by separating facts, assumptions, unknowns, role ownership, and clarification questions? Fill the required workbook fields fully: Case request; available inputs; missing information; knowns; unknowns; assumptions; role boundary; clarification questions; intake decision. Reviewer expectation: Checks whether you converted a vague workplace request into a scoped technical case with clear assumptions and questions. Submit with visible evidence, reasoning clarity, correct L2 role boundary, escalation where needed, limitation language, and artifact completeness. Do not use AI to produce the final answer; use AI only as a coach for guided questions and clarity checks. Level-specific review correction: Clarify HDL wording: the evidence pack may use either Verilog or VHDL depending on the provided module draft; you are not required to switch between both languages.", "accepted_file_types": [".xlsx", ".docx", ".pdf", ".zip"], "submission_required": true, "expected_submission_type": "file"}], "templates": [{"id": "at-emb-fpga-l2-m1", "version": 1, "file_url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/templates/emb-fpga-l2/ART_L2_M1_Simulation_Case_Intake_Template", "file_name": "ART_L2_M1_Simulation_Case_Intake_Template", "file_type": "xlsx", "artifact_id": "ma-emb-fpga-l2-m1-f", "question_id": "aq-emb-fpga-l2-m1-f1", "is_downloadable": true}], "submissions": [], "total_score": 100, "max_attempts": 3, "artifact_type": "final", "passing_score": 70, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": true}, "express": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l2-m1-p", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l2-m1-p1", "order": 1, "title": "Practice Draft: L2 Simulation Case Intake Note", "description": "Has you converted the senior engineer request into a scoped case intake by separating facts, assumptions, unknowns, role ownership, and clarification questions?", "instructions": "Draft a first pass using the full module problem and required fields: Case request; available inputs; missing information; knowns; unknowns; assumptions; role boundary; clarification questions; intake decision.. Think through the evidence manually first and use AI only as a coach for questions, clarity checks, missed-risk prompts, or limitation checks. Level-specific review correction: Clarify HDL wording: the evidence pack may use either Verilog or VHDL depending on the provided module draft; you are not required to switch between both languages.", "submission_required": false, "expected_submission_type": "text"}], "templates": [], "submissions": [], "total_score": 0, "max_attempts": null, "artifact_type": "practice", "passing_score": null, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": false}}, "knowledge": {"technical_concepts_learnt": "HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review technical focus: Case request; available inputs; missing information; knowns; unknowns; assumptions; role boundary; clarification questions; intake decision. Clarify HDL wording: the evidence pack may use either Verilog or VHDL depending on the provided module draft; you are not required to switch between both languages.", "a