nect it to simulation-validation evidence review, not final RTL correctness."}], "content_type": "explainer", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m6-explore", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m6-explore", "order": 2, "stage": "explore", "title": "Inspect evidence for L2 Simulation Portfolio Pack: 8 failing assertions; 14 waveform mismatches; 3 untested reset scenarios; 2 missing boundary-value tests; 1 valid_signal timing mismatch", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Portfolio Pack: What exact evidence should you inspect before filling this artifact?", "follow_up": "Point to the exact evidence for M6: HDL module draft, testbench, 120 stimulus cycles, waveform screenshots, simulation log, assertion summary, 8 failing assertions, 14 waveform mismatches, 3 untested reset scenarios, 2 missing boundary-value tests, and valid_signal timing mismatch."}], "content_type": "practice activity", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l2-m6-express", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l2/content/c-emb-fpga-l2-m6-express", "order": 4, "stage": "express", "title": "Template task: complete the L2 Simulation Portfolio Pack using the full module problem and required workbook fields", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L2 Simulation Portfolio Pack: What will you write in the artifact so a reviewer can verify your work?", "follow_up": "Write the artifact using M6 evidence; include facts, assumptions, owner boundary, and decision evidence."}], "content_type": "worksheet", "duration_seconds": 1200}], "support": {"ai_support_tips": "Ask AI to check whether your portfolio shows a connected evidence trail, missing-risk visibility, and transfer readiness across modules. The final portfolio must prove a controlled simulation-validation chain, not a claim that all HDL implementation behaviour is correct.", "module_continuity_link": "Uses all previous artifacts to create final portfolio proof and transfer learning for the next level or workplace review. The final portfolio must prove a controlled simulation-validation chain, not a claim that all HDL implementation behaviour is correct."}, "artifacts": {"empower": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l2-m6-f", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l2-m6-f1", "order": 1, "title": "L2 Simulation Portfolio Pack", "description": "Can a reviewer trace your final decision through all prior artifacts and see a connected evidence-based capability proof for HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review?", "instructions": "Complete the final artifact by answering the workplace artifact question: Can a reviewer trace your final decision through all prior artifacts and see a connected evidence-based capability proof for HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review? Fill the required workbook fields fully: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation. Reviewer expectation: Checks whether you connects all artifacts into a coherent capability proof. Submit with visible evidence, reasoning clarity, correct L2 role boundary, escalation where needed, limitation language, and artifact completeness. Do not use AI to produce the final answer; use AI only as a coach for guided questions and clarity checks. Level-specific review correction: The final portfolio must prove a controlled simulation-validation chain, not a claim that all HDL implementation behaviour is correct.", "accepted_file_types": [".xlsx", ".docx", ".pdf", ".zip"], "submission_required": true, "expected_submission_type": "file"}], "templates": [{"id": "at-emb-fpga-l2-m6", "version": 1, "file_url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/templates/emb-fpga-l2/ART_L2_M6_Simulation_Portfolio_Template", "file_name": "ART_L2_M6_Simulation_Portfolio_Template", "file_type": "xlsx", "artifact_id": "ma-emb-fpga-l2-m6-f", "question_id": "aq-emb-fpga-l2-m6-f1", "is_downloadable": true}], "submissions": [], "total_score": 100, "max_attempts": 3, "artifact_type": "final", "passing_score": 70, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": true}, "express": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l2-m6-p", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l2-m6-p1", "order": 1, "title": "Practice Draft: L2 Simulation Portfolio Pack", "description": "Can a reviewer trace your final decision through all prior artifacts and see a connected evidence-based capability proof for HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review?", "instructions": "Draft a first pass using the full module problem and required fields: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation.. Think through the evidence manually first and use AI only as a coach for questions, clarity checks, missed-risk prompts, or limitation checks. Level-specific review correction: The final portfolio must prove a controlled simulation-validation chain, not a claim that all HDL implementation behaviour is correct.", "submission_required": false, "expected_submission_type": "text"}], "templates": [], "submissions": [], "total_score": 0, "max_attempts": null, "artifact_type": "practice", "passing_score": null, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": false}}, "knowledge": {"technical_concepts_learnt": "HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review technical focus: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation. The final portfolio must prove a controlled simulation-validation chain, not a claim that all HDL implementation behaviour is correct.", "academic_refresher_concepts": "Portfolio structure, artifact continuity, evidence mapping, self-review, transfer reflection"}, "module_no": 6, "description": "Build the L2 Simulation Portfolio Pack by completing this L2 Embedded Design Engineer task without truncating the case context: The reviewer must see that simulation work followed evidence, not random waveform viewing. You must compile intake, validation, action, risk, closure, and corrected simulation evidence into an L2 portfolio. The final portfolio must prove a controlled simulation-validation chain, not a claim that all HDL implementation behaviour is correct.", "estimatedTime": "4 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may submit separate worksheets without proving a connected artifact chain, decision trail, and transfer readiness for the next level.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "artifact chain, evidence map, retry log, capability proof, transfer note, reviewer validation. Required workbook field focus: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation.", "reason_behind_concept": "Portfolio validation proves capability maturity by connecting every artifact into one evidence-backed decision trail. The final portfolio must prove a controlled simulation-validation chain, not a claim that all HDL implementation behaviour is correct.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L2 HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: L2 Simulation Portfolio Pack. Module problem context: The reviewer must see that simulation work followed evidence, not random waveform viewing. You must compile intake, validation, action, risk, closure, and corrected simulation evidence into an L2 portfolio. The final portfolio must prove a controlled simulation-validation chain, not a claim that all HDL implementation behaviour is correct."}, "student_confusion": "How do I prove that my final decision came from a connected evidence trail rather than isolated tasks?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L2 Portfolio, Transfer & Final Validation", "module_specific_problem_statement": "The reviewer must see that simulation work followed evidence, not random waveform viewing. You must compile intake, validation, action, risk, closure, and corrected simulation evidence into an L2 portfolio. The final portfolio must prove a controlled simulation-validation chain, not a claim that all HDL implementation behaviour is correct."}], "duration": "16 hours", "course_id": "crs-emb-fpga-l2", "xp_reward": 800, "started_at": null, "why_needed": "Embedded design engineers need HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review because workplace review decisions depend on visible evidence, not assumptions or unverified implementation confidence. This L2 course is simulation-validation focused: you review assertion and waveform evidence, classifies possible evidence categories, and does not claim final RTL correctness from simulation symptoms alone.", "course_code": "EMB-FPGA-L2", "description": "You can prepare and validate a basic HDL simulation workflow by reviewing testbench coverage, waveform mismatches, assertion failures, and simulation evidence before design review. This L2 course is simulation-validation focused: you review assertion and waveform evidence, classifies possible evidence categories, and does not claim final RTL correctness from simulation symptoms alone.", "domain_name": "Embedded Systems / FPGA Design", "level_label": "L2", "sequence_no": 2, "completed_at": null, "how_to_build": "Learners build capability by completing seven connected artifacts using the full case evidence, required workbook fields, role boundaries, risk/escalation language, and human-first engineering judgement. This L2 course is simulation-validation focused: you review assertion and waveform evidence, classifies possible evidence categories, and does not claim final RTL correctness from simulation symptoms alone.", "current_level": 1, "priority_band": "A", "thumbnail_url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1518770660439-4636190af475?q=80&w=2670&auto=format&fit=crop", "required_level": 3, "capability_name": "HDL Testbench and Simulation Validation", "difficulty_level": "beginner-intermediate", "job_pathway_role": "Embedded Design Engineer", "problem_artifacts": [{"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l2-m0", "no": 1, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet", "module": "Industry, Role & Course Readiness", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Can you explain the L2 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case? Input: use the full L2 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: HDL simulation evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Verilog/VHDL module draft; testbench with 120 stimulus cycles; waveform screenshots; simulation log; assertion summary. Evidence issues / actuals: 8 failing assertions; 14 waveform mismatches; 3 untested reset scenarios; 2 missing boundary-value tests; 1 valid_signal timing mismatch. You decision required: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Action: complete the L2 Simulation Readiness Sheet by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision. Evidence to cite: L2 simulation-validation purpose, HDL module draft in either Verilog or VHDL as supplied, testbench evidence, you role boundary, and simulation-vs-RTL-correctness limitation. Output decision: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you understands the work context before acting and can separate their own role from other engineering owners. Restriction: stay inside L2 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: Make the L2 boundary clear: simulation evidence can support review, but it does not prove final RTL correctness or implementation readiness by itself. A failing assertion or waveform mismatch is evidence to classify, not automatic proof of design failure. Consider RTL behaviour, testbench expectation, reset setup, waveform interpretation, checker alignment, or unclear expected-output logic.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l2-m1", "no": 2, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L2 Simulation Case Intake Note", "module": "Case Intake & Role Boundary", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Has you converted the senior engineer request into a scoped case intake by separating facts, assumptions, unknowns, role ownership, and clarification questions? Input: use the full L2 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: HDL simulation evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Verilog/VHDL module draft; testbench with 120 stimulus cycles; waveform screenshots; simulation log; assertion summary. Evidence issues / actuals: 8 failing assertions; 14 waveform mismatches; 3 untested reset scenarios; 2 missing boundary-value tests; 1 valid_signal timing mismatch. You decision required: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Action: complete the L2 Simulation Case Intake Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Case request; available inputs; missing information; knowns; unknowns; assumptions; role boundary; clarification questions; intake decision. Evidence to cite: senior engineer request, available HDL/testbench/waveform inputs, assertion summary, unknown expected-output logic, reset assumptions, and clarification questions. Output decision: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you converted a vague workplace request into a scoped technical case with clear assumptions and questions. Restriction: stay inside L2 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: Clarify HDL wording: the evidence pack may use either Verilog or VHDL depending on the provided module draft; you are not required to switch between both languages. A failing assertion or waveform mismatch is evidence to classify, not automatic proof of design failure. Consider RTL behaviour, testbench expectation, reset setup, waveform interpretation, checker alignment, or unclear expected-output logic.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l2-m2", "no": 3, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L2 Simulation Evidence Validation Checklist", "module": "Evidence / Requirement Validation", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Has you validated the actual engineering evidence for HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review using the required fields: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision.? Input: use the full L2 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: HDL simulation evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Verilog/VHDL module draft; testbench with 120 stimulus cycles; waveform screenshots; simulation log; assertion summary. Evidence issues / actuals: 8 failing assertions; 14 waveform mismatches; 3 untested reset scenarios; 2 missing boundary-value tests; 1 valid_signal timing mismatch. You decision required: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Action: complete the L2 Simulation Evidence Validation Checklist by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision. Evidence to cite: 120 stimulus cycles, 8 failing assertions, 14 waveform mismatches, 3 untested reset scenarios, 2 missing boundary-value tests, valid_signal timing mismatch, and expected-vs-observed evidence. Output decision: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you inspected actual evidence gaps and made a defensible proceed, retry, or block decision. Restriction: stay inside L2 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: A failing assertion or waveform mismatch is not automatically a design failure. You classify whether the issue appears linked to RTL behaviour, testbench expectation, reset setup, waveform interpretation, checker alignment, or unclear expected-output logic. A failing assertion or waveform mismatch is evidence to classify, not automatic proof of design failure. Consider RTL behaviour, testbench expectation, reset setup, waveform interpretation, checker alignment, or unclear expected-output logic.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l2-m3", "no": 4, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L2 HDL Simulation Action Note", "module": "Guided Work Action or Handoff", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Is your action or handoff note based on visible engineering evidence, documented reasoning, and a safe L2 boundary rather than unsupported fixing or overclaiming? Input: use the full L2 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: HDL simulation evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Verilog/VHDL module draft; testbench with 120 stimulus cycles; waveform screenshots; simulation log; assertion summary. Evidence issues / actuals: 8 failing assertions; 14 waveform mismatches; 3 untested reset scenarios; 2 missing boundary-value tests; 1 valid_signal timing mismatch. You decision required: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Action: complete the L2 HDL Simulation Action Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Evidence reviewed; action performed; issue counts or observations; human-first reasoning; engineering impact; handoff message; final action decision. Evidence to cite: assertion ID or mismatch ID, expected behaviour, observed behaviour, suspected category, testbench-correction need, waveform/log evidence, and recommended next step. Output decision: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you performed a concrete engineering action and made the output usable by the next stakeholder. Restriction: stay inside L2 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: You map each failing assertion or waveform mismatch to assertion ID or mismatch ID, expected behaviour, observed behaviour, suspected evidence category, recommended next step, and whether the testbench must be corrected before design review. You do not claim final RTL correctness. A failing assertion or waveform mismatch is evidence to classify, not automatic proof of design failure. Consider RTL behaviour, testbench expectation, reset setup, waveform interpretation, checker alignment, or unclear expected-output logic.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l2-m4", "no": 5, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L2 Simulation Risk and Escalation Note", "module": "Exceptions, Blockers, Risks & Escalations", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Has you identified blockers, risks, ownership gaps, severity, escalation reason, and next action using module evidence? Input: use the full L2 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: HDL simulation evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Verilog/VHDL module draft; testbench with 120 stimulus cycles; waveform screenshots; simulation log; assertion summary. Evidence issues / actuals: 8 failing assertions; 14 waveform mismatches; 3 untested reset scenarios; 2 missing boundary-value tests; 1 valid_signal timing mismatch. You decision required: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Action: complete the L2 Simulation Risk and Escalation Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Risk ID; risk category; observed evidence; consequence; severity; owner; escalation reason; recommended next action. Evidence to cite: possible RTL behaviour, testbench expectation, reset setup, waveform interpretation, checker alignment, unclear expected-output logic, severity, owner, and escalation reason. Output decision: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you links evidence to consequence, severity, owner, and escalation logic. Restriction: stay inside L2 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: You identify missing beginner-level testbench coverage, especially reset scenarios, boundary values, and expected-output checks, without creating a full verification plan. A failing assertion or waveform mismatch is evidence to classify, not automatic proof of design failure. Consider RTL behaviour, testbench expectation, reset setup, waveform interpretation, checker alignment, or unclear expected-output logic.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l2-m5", "no": 6, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L2 Simulation Quality and Closure Report", "module": "Quality, Communication & Closure", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Is the final closure message clear about what was checked, what remains limited, what decision is defensible, and what the next team should do? Input: use the full L2 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: HDL simulation evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Verilog/VHDL module draft; testbench with 120 stimulus cycles; waveform screenshots; simulation log; assertion summary. Evidence issues / actuals: 8 failing assertions; 14 waveform mismatches; 3 untested reset scenarios; 2 missing boundary-value tests; 1 valid_signal timing mismatch. You decision required: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Action: complete the L2 Simulation Quality and Closure Report by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Work completed; evidence used; checks passed; checks failed; open issues; closure message; confidence level; closure decision. Evidence to cite: simulation checks completed, failures classified, assumptions, limitations, unresolved evidence, reproducibility note, and closure decision. Output decision: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you communicate a clear, honest closure decision without overclaiming readiness. Restriction: stay inside L2 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: The closure must communicate which failures are evidence-supported, what may be testbench or expectation related, what remains uncertain, and what must be escalated before design review. A failing assertion or waveform mismatch is evidence to classify, not automatic proof of design failure. Consider RTL behaviour, testbench expectation, reset setup, waveform interpretation, checker alignment, or unclear expected-output logic.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l2-m6", "no": 7, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L2 Simulation Portfolio Pack", "module": "Portfolio, Transfer & Final Validation", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Can a reviewer trace your final decision through all prior artifacts and see a connected evidence-based capability proof for HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review? Input: use the full L2 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: HDL simulation evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Verilog/VHDL module draft; testbench with 120 stimulus cycles; waveform screenshots; simulation log; assertion summary. Evidence issues / actuals: 8 failing assertions; 14 waveform mismatches; 3 untested reset scenarios; 2 missing boundary-value tests; 1 valid_signal timing mismatch. You decision required: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Action: complete the L2 Simulation Portfolio Pack by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation. Evidence to cite: full L2 artifact chain showing controlled simulation validation, not final RTL correctness. Output decision: Simulation evidence ready / needs correction / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you connects all artifacts into a coherent capability proof. Restriction: stay inside L2 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: The final portfolio must prove a controlled simulation-validation chain, not a claim that all HDL implementation behaviour is correct. A failing assertion or waveform mismatch is evidence to classify, not automatic proof of design failure. Consider RTL behaviour, testbench expectation, reset setup, waveform interpretation, checker alignment, or unclear expected-output logic.", "status": "not_started"}], "problem_statement": {"title": "HDL Testbench and Simulation Validation for FPGA Design", "description": "After the L1 evidence-readiness review, the team accepts that the interface and design evidence is usable only with assumptions. A senior engineer now asks you to validate whether the HDL module and testbench can support a basic simulation review. You receive a Verilog/VHDL module draft, a testbench with 120 stimulus cycles, waveform screenshots, a simulation log, and an assertion summary. The evidence shows 8 failing assertions, 14 waveform mismatches, 3 untested reset scenarios, 2 missing boundary-value tests for data width, and 1 unexplained mismatch between expected and observed valid_signal timing. You must decide whether the simulation evidence can support further HDL review, needs testbench correction, or is blocked until reset, timing, and expected-output logic are clarified. This L2 course is simulation-validation focused: you review assertion and waveform evidence, classifies possible evidence categories, and does not claim final RTL correctness from simulation symptoms alone."}, "certification_value": "Portfolio-ready L2 evidence for HDL testbench and simulation validation before design review.", "completion_percentage": 0, "common_course_problem_statement": "After the L1 evidence-readiness review, the team accepts that the interface and design evidence is usable only with assumptions. A senior engineer now asks you to validate whether the HDL module and testbench can support a basic simulation review. You receive a Verilog/VHDL module draft, a testbench with 120 stimulus cycles, waveform screenshots, a simulation log, and an assertion summary. The evidence shows 8 failing assertions, 14 waveform mismatches, 3 untested reset scenarios, 2 missing boundary-value tests for data width, and 1 unexplained mismatch between expected and observed valid_signal timing. You must decide whether the simulation evidence can support further HDL review, needs testbench correction, or is blocked until reset, timing, and expected-output logic are clarified. This L2 course is simulation-validation focused: you review assertion and waveform evidence, classifies possible evidence categories, and does not claim final RTL correctness from simulation symptoms alone."}, {"id": "lpc-emb-fpga-l3", "gap": 2, "badge": "SoC Integration Readiness", "title": "FPGA SoC Integration Readiness with Embedded CPUs and Bus Interfaces", "status": "not_started", "modules": [{"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l3-m0", "title": "Industry, Role & Course Readiness", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L3_M0_Integration_Readiness_Template; you evidence inputs: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log; workbook field focus: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision.", "tool_type_free_or_paid": "template + evidence pack"}, "stages": [{"key": "engage", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explore", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explain", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "express", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "empower", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "evolve", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}], "status": "available", "content": [{"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-empower", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-empower", "order": 5, "stage": "empower", "title": "Decision check for L3 Integration Readiness Sheet: defend L3 readiness using visible evidence and limitations", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Readiness Sheet: What decision can you defend from the evidence at this stage?", "follow_up": "Make the decision yourself using evidence: ready for integration planning, partially ready with handoff gaps, or blocked until interface-owner confirmation; do not ask AI to decide for you."}], "content_type": "checklist", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-engage", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-engage", "order": 1, "stage": "engage", "title": "L3 L3 Integration Readiness Sheet kickoff: workplace risk, role boundary, and why SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs cannot be assumed", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Readiness Sheet: What workplace risk appears in this module before you propose any action?", "follow_up": "Name the workplace risk in M0 using integration-readiness handoff, not SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt, or high-speed interface redesign language before suggesting any action."}], "content_type": "case brief", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-evolve", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-evolve", "order": 6, "stage": "evolve", "title": "Reflection for L3 Integration Readiness Sheet: transfer this reasoning to the next embedded FPGA level or workplace case", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Readiness Sheet: How does this module prepare you for the next module, next level, or another embedded FPGA case?", "follow_up": "State one reusable rule for moving from this level toward L4 timing and performance risk review without overclaiming responsibility."}], "content_type": "reflection prompt", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-explain", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-explain", "order": 3, "stage": "explain", "title": "Guide for L3 Integration Readiness Sheet: concepts, ownership boundary, and safe you decision language", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Readiness Sheet: Which concept or boundary explains why this evidence matters?", "follow_up": "Explain the key concept in simple engineering language and connect it to integration-readiness handoff, not SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt, or high-speed interface redesign."}], "content_type": "explainer", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-explore", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-explore", "order": 2, "stage": "explore", "title": "Inspect evidence for L3 Integration Readiness Sheet: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Readiness Sheet: What exact evidence should you inspect before filling this artifact?", "follow_up": "Point to the exact evidence for M0: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, register fields, software-access note, integration issue log, and lane-mapping gap."}], "content_type": "practice activity", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-express", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m0-express", "order": 4, "stage": "express", "title": "Template task: complete the L3 Integration Readiness Sheet using the full module problem and required workbook fields", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Readiness Sheet: What will you write in the artifact so a reviewer can verify your work?", "follow_up": "Write the artifact using M0 evidence; include facts, assumptions, owner boundary, and decision evidence."}], "content_type": "worksheet", "duration_seconds": 1200}], "support": {"ai_support_tips": "Ask AI to explain the L3 boundary in simple terms, then rewrite the role responsibility in your own words before filling the readiness sheet. Use platform terms carefully: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and integration issue log are the core evidence; examples such as ARM/NIOS, AXI/Avalon, or DDR/QDR depend on the actual platform.", "module_continuity_link": "Creates the readiness context that feeds Module 1 case intake. Use platform terms carefully: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and integration issue log are the core evidence; examples such as ARM/NIOS, AXI/Avalon, or DDR/QDR depend on the actual platform."}, "artifacts": {"empower": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m0-f", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m0-f1", "order": 1, "title": "L3 Integration Readiness Sheet", "description": "Can you explain the L3 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case?", "instructions": "Complete the final artifact by answering the workplace artifact question: Can you explain the L3 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case? Fill the required workbook fields fully: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision. Reviewer expectation: Checks whether you understands the work context before acting and can separate their own role from other engineering owners. Submit with visible evidence, reasoning clarity, correct L3 role boundary, escalation where needed, limitation language, and artifact completeness. Do not use AI to produce the final answer; use AI only as a coach for guided questions and clarity checks. Level-specific review correction: Use platform terms carefully: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and integration issue log are the core evidence; examples such as ARM/NIOS, AXI/Avalon, or DDR/QDR depend on the actual platform.", "accepted_file_types": [".xlsx", ".docx", ".pdf", ".zip"], "submission_required": true, "expected_submission_type": "file"}], "templates": [{"id": "at-emb-fpga-l3-m0", "version": 1, "file_url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/templates/emb-fpga-l3/ART_L3_M0_Integration_Readiness_Template", "file_name": "ART_L3_M0_Integration_Readiness_Template", "file_type": "xlsx", "artifact_id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m0-f", "question_id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m0-f1", "is_downloadable": true}], "submissions": [], "total_score": 100, "max_attempts": 3, "artifact_type": "final", "passing_score": 70, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": true}, "express": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m0-p", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m0-p1", "order": 1, "title": "Practice Draft: L3 Integration Readiness Sheet", "description": "Can you explain the L3 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case?", "instructions": "Draft a first pass using the full module problem and required fields: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision.. Think through the evidence manually first and use AI only as a coach for questions, clarity checks, missed-risk prompts, or limitation checks. Level-specific review correction: Use platform terms carefully: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and integration issue log are the core evidence; examples such as ARM/NIOS, AXI/Avalon, or DDR/QDR depend on the actual platform.", "submission_required": false, "expected_submission_type": "text"}], "templates": [], "submissions": [], "total_score": 0, "max_attempts": null, "artifact_type": "practice", "passing_score": null, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": false}}, "knowledge": {"technical_concepts_learnt": "SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs technical focus: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision. Use platform terms carefully: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and integration issue log are the core evidence; examples such as ARM/NIOS, AXI/Avalon, or DDR/QDR depend on the actual platform.", "academic_refresher_concepts": "Embedded systems workflow, FPGA/HDL review basics, role responsibility, evidence readiness"}, "module_no": 0, "description": "Build the L3 Integration Readiness Sheet by completing this L3 Embedded Design Engineer task without truncating the case context: You are moving from simulation validation to SoC integration readiness. The risk is that individually simulated blocks may fail when connected through CPU access, bus interfaces, memory mapping, interrupts, and high-speed lanes. You must understand how IP blocks, bus, CPU, memory, and ownership handoffs connect. Use platform terms carefully: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and integration issue log are the core evidence; examples such as CPU, bus, or memory depend on the actual platform.", "estimatedTime": "2 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may start technical work without understanding why SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs depends on evidence readiness, role scope, and ownership boundaries.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "work context, role boundary, evidence readiness, non-owned dependencies, readiness decision. Required workbook field focus: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision.", "reason_behind_concept": "Readiness understanding prevents you from treating a limited evidence pack as a complete engineering approval. Use platform terms carefully: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and integration issue log are the core evidence; examples such as ARM/NIOS, AXI/Avalon, or DDR/QDR depend on the actual platform.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L3 SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: L3 Integration Readiness Sheet. Module problem context: You are moving from simulation validation to SoC integration readiness. The risk is that individually simulated blocks may fail when connected through CPU access, bus interfaces, memory mapping, interrupts, and high-speed lanes. You must understand how IP blocks, AXI/Avalon, ARM/NIOS, DDR/QDR, and ownership handoffs connect. Use platform terms carefully: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and integration issue log are the core evidence; examples such as ARM/NIOS, AXI/Avalon, or DDR/QDR depend on the actual platform."}, "student_confusion": "What does L3 mean here, and am I supposed to solve the full embedded design problem or validate readiness evidence?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L3 Industry, Role & Course Readiness", "module_specific_problem_statement": "You are moving from simulation validation to SoC integration readiness. The risk is that individually simulated blocks may fail when connected through CPU access, bus interfaces, memory mapping, interrupts, and high-speed lanes. You must understand how IP blocks, bus, CPU, memory, and ownership handoffs connect. Use platform terms carefully: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and integration issue log are the core evidence; examples such as CPU, bus, or memory depend on the actual platform."}, {"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l3-m1", "title": "Case Intake & Role Boundary", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L3_M1_Integration_Case_Intake_Template; you evidence inputs: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log; workbook field focus: Case request; available inputs; missing information; knowns; unknowns; assumptions; role boundary; clarification questions; intake decision.", "tool_type_free_or_paid": "template + evidence pack"}, "stages": [{"key": "engage", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explore", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explain", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "express", "status": "not_started", 