e L3 Integration Case Intake Note as input and produces the L3 SoC Evidence Validation Checklist that feeds the L3 Interface Handoff Note. Make software-access alignment visible: you checks whether register fields, address map, CPU access note, and software-facing documentation agree before integration planning continues."}, "artifacts": {"empower": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m2-f", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m2-f1", "order": 1, "title": "L3 SoC Evidence Validation Checklist", "description": "Has you validated the actual engineering evidence for SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs using the required fields: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision.?", "instructions": "Complete the final artifact by answering the workplace artifact question: Has you validated the actual engineering evidence for SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs using the required fields: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision.? Fill the required workbook fields fully: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision. Reviewer expectation: Checks whether you inspected actual evidence gaps and made a defensible proceed, retry, or block decision. Submit with visible evidence, reasoning clarity, correct L3 role boundary, escalation where needed, limitation language, and artifact completeness. Do not use AI to produce the final answer; use AI only as a coach for guided questions and clarity checks. Level-specific review correction: Make software-access alignment visible: you checks whether register fields, address map, CPU access note, and software-facing documentation agree before integration planning continues.", "accepted_file_types": [".xlsx", ".docx", ".pdf", ".zip"], "submission_required": true, "expected_submission_type": "file"}], "templates": [{"id": "at-emb-fpga-l3-m2", "version": 1, "file_url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/templates/emb-fpga-l3/ART_L3_M2_SoC_Evidence_Validation_Template", "file_name": "ART_L3_M2_SoC_Evidence_Validation_Template", "file_type": "xlsx", "artifact_id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m2-f", "question_id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m2-f1", "is_downloadable": true}], "submissions": [], "total_score": 100, "max_attempts": 3, "artifact_type": "final", "passing_score": 70, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": true}, "express": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m2-p", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m2-p1", "order": 1, "title": "Practice Draft: L3 SoC Evidence Validation Checklist", "description": "Has you validated the actual engineering evidence for SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs using the required fields: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision.?", "instructions": "Draft a first pass using the full module problem and required fields: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision.. Think through the evidence manually first and use AI only as a coach for questions, clarity checks, missed-risk prompts, or limitation checks. Level-specific review correction: Make software-access alignment visible: you checks whether register fields, address map, CPU access note, and software-facing documentation agree before integration planning continues.", "submission_required": false, "expected_submission_type": "text"}], "templates": [], "submissions": [], "total_score": 0, "max_attempts": null, "artifact_type": "practice", "passing_score": null, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": false}}, "knowledge": {"technical_concepts_learnt": "SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs technical focus: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision. Make software-access alignment visible: you checks whether register fields, address map, CPU access note, and software-facing documentation agree before integration planning continues.", "academic_refresher_concepts": "Checklist validation, evidence quality, mismatch counting, requirement trace, readiness status"}, "module_no": 2, "description": "Build the L3 SoC Evidence Validation Checklist by completing this L3 Embedded Design Engineer task without truncating the case context: You validate the integration evidence: 4 IP blocks, an CPU CPU access note, an bus bus mapping table, memory memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and an integration issue log. The evidence shows 2 bus-address conflicts, 3 memory-timing warnings, 1 interrupt line without owner, 2 register fields misaligned with software access, and 1 high-speed lane mapping gap. Make software-access alignment visible: you checks whether register fields, address map, CPU access note, and software-facing documentation agree before integration planning continues.", "estimatedTime": "2 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may treat incomplete or inconsistent evidence as review-ready even though the case contains: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "evidence completeness, consistency, mismatch/gap count, source ownership, readiness impact. Required workbook field focus: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision.", "reason_behind_concept": "Validation is required because the supplied evidence includes measurable gaps and actuals that affect the safety of the next review step. Make software-access alignment visible: you checks whether register fields, address map, CPU access note, and software-facing documentation agree before integration planning continues.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L3 SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: L3 SoC Evidence Validation Checklist. Module problem context: You validate the integration evidence: 4 IP blocks, an ARM/NIOS CPU access note, an AXI/Avalon bus mapping table, DDR/QDR memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and an integration issue log. The evidence shows 2 bus-address conflicts, 3 memory-timing warnings, 1 interrupt line without owner, 2 register fields misaligned with software access, and 1 high-speed lane mapping gap. Make software-access alignment visible: you checks whether register fields, address map, CPU access note, and software-facing documentation agree before integration planning continues."}, "student_confusion": "How do I know whether the evidence is complete, consistent, and trustworthy enough for the next engineering step?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L3 Evidence / Requirement Validation", "module_specific_problem_statement": "You validate the integration evidence: 4 IP blocks, an CPU CPU access note, an bus bus mapping table, memory memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and an integration issue log. The evidence shows 2 bus-address conflicts, 3 memory-timing warnings, 1 interrupt line without owner, 2 register fields misaligned with software access, and 1 high-speed lane mapping gap. Make software-access alignment visible: you checks whether register fields, address map, CPU access note, and software-facing documentation agree before integration planning continues."}, {"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l3-m3", "title": "Guided Work Action or Handoff", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L3_M3_Interface_Handoff_Template; you evidence inputs: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log; workbook field focus: Evidence reviewed; action performed; issue counts or observations; human-first reasoning; engineering impact; handoff message; final action decision.", "tool_type_free_or_paid": "template + evidence pack"}, "stages": [{"key": "engage", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explore", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explain", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "express", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "empower", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "evolve", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}], "status": "available", "content": [{"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-empower", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-empower", "order": 5, "stage": "empower", "title": "Decision check for L3 Interface Handoff Note: defend L3 readiness using visible evidence and limitations", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Interface Handoff Note: What decision can you defend from the evidence at this stage?", "follow_up": "Make the decision yourself using evidence: ready for integration planning, partially ready with handoff gaps, or blocked until interface-owner confirmation; do not ask AI to decide for you."}], "content_type": "checklist", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-engage", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-engage", "order": 1, "stage": "engage", "title": "L3 L3 Interface Handoff Note kickoff: workplace risk, role boundary, and why SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs cannot be assumed", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Interface Handoff Note: What workplace risk appears in this module before you propose any action?", "follow_up": "Name the workplace risk in M3 using integration-readiness handoff, not SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt, or high-speed interface redesign language before suggesting any action."}], "content_type": "case brief", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-evolve", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-evolve", "order": 6, "stage": "evolve", "title": "Reflection for L3 Interface Handoff Note: transfer this reasoning to the next embedded FPGA level or workplace case", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Interface Handoff Note: How does this module prepare you for the next module, next level, or another embedded FPGA case?", "follow_up": "State one reusable rule for moving from this level toward L4 timing and performance risk review without overclaiming responsibility."}], "content_type": "reflection prompt", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-explain", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-explain", "order": 3, "stage": "explain", "title": "Guide for L3 Interface Handoff Note: concepts, ownership boundary, and safe you decision language", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Interface Handoff Note: Which concept or boundary explains why this evidence matters?", "follow_up": "Explain the key concept in simple engineering language and connect it to integration-readiness handoff, not SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt, or high-speed interface redesign."}], "content_type": "explainer", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-explore", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-explore", "order": 2, "stage": "explore", "title": "Inspect evidence for L3 Interface Handoff Note: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Interface Handoff Note: What exact evidence should you inspect before filling this artifact?", "follow_up": "Point to the exact evidence for M3: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, register fields, software-access note, integration issue log, and lane-mapping gap."}], "content_type": "practice activity", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-express", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m3-express", "order": 4, "stage": "express", "title": "Template task: complete the L3 Interface Handoff Note using the full module problem and required workbook fields", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Interface Handoff Note: What will you write in the artifact so a reviewer can verify your work?", "follow_up": "Include register/address agreement, CPU/software access alignment, missing interface fields, owner, and handoff message."}], "content_type": "worksheet", "duration_seconds": 1200}], "support": {"ai_support_tips": "Ask AI to review whether your action or handoff note is evidence-based, reproducible, and level-safe without asking it to create the final answer. You map bus conflicts, register-field mismatches, interrupt ownership gaps, memory-interface warnings, and lane-mapping gaps into an integration handoff note. You do not redesign the SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface.", "module_continuity_link": "Uses the L3 SoC Evidence Validation Checklist as input and produces the L3 Interface Handoff Note that feeds the L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note. You map bus conflicts, register-field mismatches, interrupt ownership gaps, memory-interface warnings, and lane-mapping gaps into an integration handoff note. You do not redesign the SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface."}, "artifacts": {"empower": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m3-f", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m3-f1", "order": 1, "title": "L3 Interface Handoff Note", "description": "Is your action or handoff note based on visible engineering evidence, documented reasoning, and a safe L3 boundary rather than unsupported fixing or overclaiming?", "instructions": "Complete the final artifact by answering the workplace artifact question: Is your action or handoff note based on visible engineering evidence, documented reasoning, and a safe L3 boundary rather than unsupported fixing or overclaiming? Fill the required workbook fields fully: Evidence reviewed; action performed; issue counts or observations; human-first reasoning; engineering impact; handoff message; final action decision. Reviewer expectation: Checks whether you performed a concrete engineering action and made the output usable by the next stakeholder. Submit with visible evidence, reasoning clarity, correct L3 role boundary, escalation where needed, limitation language, and artifact completeness. Do not use AI to produce the final answer; use AI only as a coach for guided questions and clarity checks. Level-specific review correction: You map bus conflicts, register-field mismatches, interrupt ownership gaps, memory-interface warnings, and lane-mapping gaps into an integration handoff note. You do not redesign the SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface.", "accepted_file_types": [".xlsx", ".docx", ".pdf", ".zip"], "submission_required": true, "expected_submission_type": "file"}], "templates": [{"id": "at-emb-fpga-l3-m3", "version": 1, "file_url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/templates/emb-fpga-l3/ART_L3_M3_Interface_Handoff_Template", "file_name": "ART_L3_M3_Interface_Handoff_Template", "file_type": "xlsx", "artifact_id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m3-f", "question_id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m3-f1", "is_downloadable": true}], "submissions": [], "total_score": 100, "max_attempts": 3, "artifact_type": "final", "passing_score": 70, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": true}, "express": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m3-p", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m3-p1", "order": 1, "title": "Practice Draft: L3 Interface Handoff Note", "description": "Is your action or handoff note based on visible engineering evidence, documented reasoning, and a safe L3 boundary rather than unsupported fixing or overclaiming?", "instructions": "Draft a first pass using the full module problem and required fields: Evidence reviewed; action performed; issue counts or observations; human-first reasoning; engineering impact; handoff message; final action decision.. Think through the evidence manually first and use AI only as a coach for questions, clarity checks, missed-risk prompts, or limitation checks. Level-specific review correction: You map bus conflicts, register-field mismatches, interrupt ownership gaps, memory-interface warnings, and lane-mapping gaps into an integration handoff note. You do not redesign the SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface.", "submission_required": false, "expected_submission_type": "text"}], "templates": [], "submissions": [], "total_score": 0, "max_attempts": null, "artifact_type": "practice", "passing_score": null, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": false}}, "knowledge": {"technical_concepts_learnt": "SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs technical focus: Evidence reviewed; action performed; issue counts or observations; human-first reasoning; engineering impact; handoff message; final action decision. You map bus conflicts, register-field mismatches, interrupt ownership gaps, memory-interface warnings, and lane-mapping gaps into an integration handoff note. You do not redesign the SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface.", "academic_refresher_concepts": "Engineering handoff writing, action notes, reproducibility, human-first reasoning, decision logging"}, "module_no": 3, "description": "Build the L3 Interface Handoff Note by completing this L3 Embedded Design Engineer task without truncating the case context: You must create an interface handoff by mapping IP blocks to bus addresses, marking register-field mismatches, listing memory-interface warnings, documenting interrupt ownership gaps, and producing a handoff that software, embedded, and FPGA teams can use for integration planning. You map bus conflicts, register-field mismatches, interrupt ownership gaps, memory-interface warnings, and lane-mapping gaps into an integration handoff note. You do not redesign the SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface.", "estimatedTime": "2 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may create the L3 Interface Handoff Note as a generic note instead of making a concrete evidence-based action or handoff that another engineer can use.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "evidence-based action, handoff logic, human-first reasoning, level-safe engineering decision. Required workbook field focus: Evidence reviewed; action performed; issue counts or observations; human-first reasoning; engineering impact; handoff message; final action decision.", "reason_behind_concept": "A usable action or handoff note must show exactly what evidence was reviewed, what was done, and what decision is still limited. You map bus conflicts, register-field mismatches, interrupt ownership gaps, memory-interface warnings, and lane-mapping gaps into an integration handoff note. You do not redesign the SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L3 SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: L3 Interface Handoff Note. Module problem context: You must create an interface handoff by mapping IP blocks to bus addresses, marking register-field mismatches, listing memory-interface warnings, documenting interrupt ownership gaps, and producing a handoff that software, embedded, and FPGA teams can use for integration planning. You map bus conflicts, register-field mismatches, interrupt ownership gaps, memory-interface warnings, and lane-mapping gaps into an integration handoff note. You do not redesign the SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface."}, "student_confusion": "Should I fix the issue, document it, hand it off, or escalate it based on my level boundary?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L3 Guided Work Action or Handoff", "module_specific_problem_statement": "You must create an interface handoff by mapping IP blocks to bus addresses, marking register-field mismatches, listing memory-interface warnings, documenting interrupt ownership gaps, and producing a handoff that software, embedded, and FPGA teams can use for integration planning. You map bus conflicts, register-field mismatches, interrupt ownership gaps, memory-interface warnings, and lane-mapping gaps into an integration handoff note. You do not redesign the SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface."}, {"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l3-m4", "title": "Exceptions, Blockers, Risks & Escalations", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L3_M4_Integration_Risk_Escalation_Template; you evidence inputs: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log; workbook field focus: Risk ID; risk category; observed e