design language before suggesting any action."}], "content_type": "case brief", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m4-evolve", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m4-evolve", "order": 6, "stage": "evolve", "title": "Reflection for L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note: transfer this reasoning to the next embedded FPGA level or workplace case", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note: How does this module prepare you for the next module, next level, or another embedded FPGA case?", "follow_up": "State one reusable rule for moving from this level toward L4 timing and performance risk review without overclaiming responsibility."}], "content_type": "reflection prompt", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m4-explain", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m4-explain", "order": 3, "stage": "explain", "title": "Guide for L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note: concepts, ownership boundary, and safe you decision language", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note: Which concept or boundary explains why this evidence matters?", "follow_up": "Explain the key concept in simple engineering language and connect it to integration-readiness handoff, not SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt, or high-speed interface redesign."}], "content_type": "explainer", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m4-explore", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m4-explore", "order": 2, "stage": "explore", "title": "Inspect evidence for L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note: What exact evidence should you inspect before filling this artifact?", "follow_up": "Point to the exact evidence for M4: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, register fields, software-access note, integration issue log, and lane-mapping gap."}], "content_type": "practice activity", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m4-express", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m4-express", "order": 4, "stage": "express", "title": "Template task: complete the L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note using the full module problem and required workbook fields", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note: What will you write in the artifact so a reviewer can verify your work?", "follow_up": "Write the artifact using M4 evidence; include bus conflicts, memory timing warnings, interrupt ownership, software-access mismatch, and lane mapping."}], "content_type": "worksheet", "duration_seconds": 1200}], "support": {"ai_support_tips": "Ask AI to help separate blocker, risk, owner, and escalation categories, then choose severity using the visible evidence yourself. High-speed lane mapping gaps are escalation risks requiring senior or interface-owner confirmation. Memory timing warnings and interrupt ownership gaps should be classified as handoff or blocker items, not you-owned fixes.", "module_continuity_link": "Uses the L3 Interface Handoff Note as input and produces the L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note that feeds the L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report. High-speed lane mapping gaps are escalation risks requiring senior or interface-owner confirmation. Memory timing warnings and interrupt ownership gaps should be classified as handoff or blocker items, not you-owned fixes."}, "artifacts": {"empower": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m4-f", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m4-f1", "order": 1, "title": "L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note", "description": "Has you identified blockers, risks, ownership gaps, severity, escalation reason, and next action using module evidence?", "instructions": "Complete the final artifact by answering the workplace artifact question: Has you identified blockers, risks, ownership gaps, severity, escalation reason, and next action using module evidence? Fill the required workbook fields fully: Risk ID; risk category; observed evidence; consequence; severity; owner; escalation reason; recommended next action. Reviewer expectation: Checks whether you links evidence to consequence, severity, owner, and escalation logic. Submit with visible evidence, reasoning clarity, correct L3 role boundary, escalation where needed, limitation language, and artifact completeness. Do not use AI to produce the final answer; use AI only as a coach for guided questions and clarity checks. Level-specific review correction: High-speed lane mapping gaps are escalation risks requiring senior or interface-owner confirmation. Memory timing warnings and interrupt ownership gaps should be classified as handoff or blocker items, not you-owned fixes.", "accepted_file_types": [".xlsx", ".docx", ".pdf", ".zip"], "submission_required": true, "expected_submission_type": "file"}], "templates": [{"id": "at-emb-fpga-l3-m4", "version": 1, "file_url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/templates/emb-fpga-l3/ART_L3_M4_Integration_Risk_Escalation_Template", "file_name": "ART_L3_M4_Integration_Risk_Escalation_Template", "file_type": "xlsx", "artifact_id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m4-f", "question_id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m4-f1", "is_downloadable": true}], "submissions": [], "total_score": 100, "max_attempts": 3, "artifact_type": "final", "passing_score": 70, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": true}, "express": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m4-p", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m4-p1", "order": 1, "title": "Practice Draft: L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note", "description": "Has you identified blockers, risks, ownership gaps, severity, escalation reason, and next action using module evidence?", "instructions": "Draft a first pass using the full module problem and required fields: Risk ID; risk category; observed evidence; consequence; severity; owner; escalation reason; recommended next action.. Think through the evidence manually first and use AI only as a coach for questions, clarity checks, missed-risk prompts, or limitation checks. Level-specific review correction: High-speed lane mapping gaps are escalation risks requiring senior or interface-owner confirmation. Memory timing warnings and interrupt ownership gaps should be classified as handoff or blocker items, not you-owned fixes.", "submission_required": false, "expected_submission_type": "text"}], "templates": [], "submissions": [], "total_score": 0, "max_attempts": null, "artifact_type": "practice", "passing_score": null, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": false}}, "knowledge": {"technical_concepts_learnt": "SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs technical focus: Risk ID; risk category; observed evidence; consequence; severity; owner; escalation reason; recommended next action. High-speed lane mapping gaps are escalation risks requiring senior or interface-owner confirmation. Memory timing warnings and interrupt ownership gaps should be classified as handoff or blocker items, not you-owned fixes.", "academic_refresher_concepts": "Risk categories, severity, escalation logic, owner mapping, next-action planning"}, "module_no": 4, "description": "Build the L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note by completing this L3 Embedded Design Engineer task without truncating the case context: You identify integration risks: bus conflicts may cause incorrect CPU reads/writes, memory warnings may destabilize data transfer, interrupt ownership gaps may break event handling, register mismatch may create software defects, and high-speed lane gaps may delay interface validation. You must classify and escalate these risks. High-speed lane mapping gaps are escalation risks requiring senior or interface-owner confirmation. Memory timing warnings and interrupt ownership gaps should be classified as handoff or blocker items, not you-owned fixes.", "estimatedTime": "2 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may ignore which issues are blockers, which are manageable, and which owner must resolve them before the next engineering step.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "risk category, consequence, severity, escalation owner, recommended next action. Required workbook field focus: Risk ID; risk category; observed evidence; consequence; severity; owner; escalation reason; recommended next action.", "reason_behind_concept": "Risk classification makes blockers and escalation ownership visible before the design, simulation, integration, timing, or validation path proceeds. High-speed lane mapping gaps are escalation risks requiring senior or interface-owner confirmation. Memory timing warnings and interrupt ownership gaps should be classified as handoff or blocker items, not you-owned fixes.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L3 SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note. Module problem context: You identify integration risks: bus conflicts may cause incorrect CPU reads/writes, memory warnings may destabilize data transfer, interrupt ownership gaps may break event handling, register mismatch may create software defects, and high-speed lane gaps may delay interface validation. You must classify and escalate these risks. High-speed lane mapping gaps are escalation risks requiring senior or interface-owner confirmation. Memory timing warnings and interrupt ownership gaps should be classified as handoff or blocker items, not you-owned fixes."}, "student_confusion": "Which observations are minor, which are risks, and which are blockers that need owner confirmation?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L3 Exceptions, Blockers, Risks & Escalations", "module_specific_problem_statement": "You identify integration risks: bus conflicts may cause incorrect CPU reads/writes, memory warnings may destabilize data transfer, interrupt ownership gaps may break event handling, register mismatch may create software defects, and high-speed lane gaps may delay interface validation. You must classify and escalate these risks. High-speed lane mapping gaps are escalation risks requiring senior or interface-owner confirmation. Memory timing warnings and interrupt ownership gaps should be classified as handoff or blocker items, not you-owned fixes."}, {"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l3-m5", "title": "Quality, Communication & Closure", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L3_M5_Integration_Quality_Closure_Template; you evidence inputs: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log; workbook field focus: Work completed; evidence used; checks passed; checks failed; open issues; closure message; confidence level; closure decision.", "tool_type_free_or_paid": "template + evidence pack"}, "stages": [{"key": "engage", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explore", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explain", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "express", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "empower", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "evolve", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}], "status": "available", "content": [{"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-empower", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-empower", "order": 5, "stage": "empower", "title": "Decision check for L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report: defend L3 readiness using visible evidence and limitations", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report: What decision can you defend from the evidence at this stage?", "follow_up": "Make the decision yourself using evidence: ready for integration planning, partially ready with handoff gaps, or blocked until interface-owner confirmation; do not ask AI to decide for you."}], "content_type": "checklist", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-engage", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-engage", "order": 1, "stage": "engage", "title": "L3 L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report kickoff: workplace risk, role boundary, and why SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs cannot be assumed", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report: What workplace risk appears in this module before you propose any action?", "follow_up": "Name the workplace risk in M5 using integration-readiness handoff, not SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt, or high-speed interface redesign language before suggesting any action."}], "content_type": "case brief", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-evolve", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-evolve", "order": 6, "stage": "evolve", "title": "Reflection for L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report: transfer this reasoning to the next embedded FPGA level or workplace case", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report: How does this module prepare you for the next module, next level, or another embedded FPGA case?", "follow_up": "State one reusable rule for moving from this level toward L4 timing and performance risk review without overclaiming responsibility."}], "content_type": "reflection prompt", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-explain", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-explain", "order": 3, "stage": "explain", "title": "Guide for L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report: concepts, ownership boundary, and safe you decision language", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report: Which concept or boundary explains why this evidence matters?", "follow_up": "Explain the key concept in simple engineering language and connect it to integration-readiness handoff, not SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt, or high-speed interface redesign."}], "content_type": "explainer", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-explore", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-explore", "order": 2, "stage": "explore", "title": "Inspect evidence for L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report: What exact evidence should you inspect before filling this artifact?", "follow_up": "Point to the exact evidence for M5: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, register fields, software-access note, integration issue log, and lane-mapping gap."}], "content_type": "practice activity", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-express", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l3/content/c-emb-fpga-l3-m5-express", "order": 4, "stage": "express", "title": "Template task: complete the L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report using the full module problem and required workbook fields", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report: What will you write in the artifact so a reviewer can verify your work?", "follow_up": "Write the artifact using M5 evidence; include facts, assumptions, owner boundary, and decision evidence."}], "content_type": "worksheet", "duration_seconds": 1200}], "support": {"ai_support_tips": "Ask AI to check whether your closure message is clear, limitation-aware, and professional for a senior engineer reviewer. The closure must state whether the evidence is usable for integration planning, partially ready with interface changes, or blocked until software-access, memory, interrupt, or lane-mapping gaps are resolved.", "module_continuity_link": "Uses the L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note as input and produces the L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report that feeds the L3 Integration Portfolio Pack. The closure must state whether the evidence is usable for integration planning, partially ready with interface changes, or blocked until software-access, memory, interrupt, or lane-mapping gaps are resolved."}, "artifacts": {"empower": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m5-f", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m5-f1", "order": 1, "title": "L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report", "description": "Is the final closure message clear about what was checked, what remains limited, what decision is defensible, and what the next team should do?", "instructions": "Complete the final artifact by answering the workplace artifact question: Is the final closure message clear about what was checked, what remains limited, what decision is defensible, and what the next team should do? Fill the required workbook fields fully: Work completed; evidence used; checks passed; checks failed; open issues; closure message; confidence level; closure decision. Reviewer expectation: Checks whether you communicate a clear, honest closure decision without overclaiming readiness. Submit with visible evidence, reasoning clarity, correct L3 role boundary, escalation where needed, limitation language, and artifact completeness. Do not use AI to produce the final answer; use AI only as a coach for guided questions and clarity checks. Level-specific review correction: The closure must state whether the evidence is usable for integration planning, partially ready with interface changes, or blocked until software-access, memory, interrupt, or lane-mapping gaps are resolved.", "accepted_file_types": [".xlsx", ".docx", ".pdf", ".zip"], "submission_required": true, "expected_submission_type": "file"}], "templates": [{"id": "at-emb-fpga-l3-m5", "version": 1, "file_url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/templates/emb-fpga-l3/ART_L3_M5_Integration_Quality_Closure_Template", "file_name": "ART_L3_M5_Integration_Quality_Closure_Template", "file_type": "xlsx", "artifact_id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m5-f", "question_id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m5-f1", "is_downloadable": true}], "submissions": [], "total_score": 100, "max_attempts": 3, "artifact_type": "final", "passing_score": 70, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": true}, "express": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m5-p", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m5-p1", "order": 1, "title": "Practice Draft: L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report", "description": "Is the final closure message clear about what was checked, what remains limited, what decision is defensible, and what the next team should do?", "instructions": "Draft a first pass using the full module problem and required fields: Work completed; evidence used; checks passed; checks failed; open issues; closure message; confidence level; closure decision.. Think through the evidence manually first and use AI only as a coach for questions, clarity checks, missed-risk prompts, or limitation checks. Level-specific review correction: The closure must state whether the evidence is usable for integration planning, partially ready with interface changes, or blocked until software-access, memory, interrupt, or lane-mapping gaps are resolved.", "submission_required": false, "expected_submission_type": "text"}], "templates": [], "submissions": [], "total_score": 0, "max_attempts": null, "artifact_type": "practice", "passing_score": null, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": false}}, "knowledge": {"technical_concepts_learnt": "SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs technical focus: Work completed; evidence used; checks passed; checks failed; open issues; closure message; confidence level; closure decision. The closure must state whether the evidence is usable for integration planning, partially ready with interface changes, or blocked until software-access, memory, interrupt, or lane-mapping gaps are resolved.", "academic_refresher_concepts": "Technical closure, reviewer communication, limitation wording, confidence level, decision summary"}, "module_no": 5, "description": "Build the L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report by completing this L3 Embedded Design Engineer task without truncating the case context: The senior engineer needs an integration-readiness closure. You must state what is ready for planning, what requires correction, what is blocked, and which owners must resolve each integration dependency. The closure must state whether the evidence is usable for integration planning, partially ready with interface changes, or blocked until software-access, memory, interrupt, or lane-mapping gaps are resolved.", "estimatedTime": "2 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may communicate a vague or overconfident closure decision without stating evidence strength, remaining limitations, and next action.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "quality check, closure communication, confidence level, limitation language, next-step decision. Required workbook field focus: Work completed; evidence used; checks passed; checks failed; open issues; closure message; confidence level; closure decision.", "reason_behind_concept": "Closure language helps senior engineers understand the decision without confusing partial readiness with full approval. The closure must state whether the evidence is usable for integration planning, partially ready with interface changes, or blocked until software-access, memory, interrupt, or lane-mapping gaps are resolved.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L3 SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report. Module problem context: The senior engineer needs an integration-readiness closure. You must state what is ready for planning, what requires correction, what is blocked, and which owners must resolve each integration dependency. The closure must state whether the evidence is usable for integration planning, partially ready with interface changes, or blocked until software-access, memory, interrupt, or lane-mapping gaps are resolved."}, "student_confusion": "How do I communicate a technical decision clearly without overclaiming readiness or hiding limitations?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L3 Quality, Communication & Closure", "module_specific_problem_statement": "The senior engineer needs an integration-readiness closure. You must state what is ready for planning, what requires correction, what is blocked, and which owners must resolve each integration dependency. The closure must state whether the evidence is usable for integration planning, partially ready with interface changes, or blocked until software-access, memory, interrupt, or lane-mapping gaps are resolved."}, {"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l3-m6", "title": "Portfolio, Transfer & F