to timing/performance review."}, "artifacts": {"empower": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m6-f", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m6-f1", "order": 1, "title": "L3 Integration Portfolio Pack", "description": "Can a reviewer trace your final decision through all prior artifacts and see a connected evidence-based capability proof for SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs?", "instructions": "Complete the final artifact by answering the workplace artifact question: Can a reviewer trace your final decision through all prior artifacts and see a connected evidence-based capability proof for SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs? Fill the required workbook fields fully: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation. Reviewer expectation: Checks whether you connects all artifacts into a coherent capability proof. Submit with visible evidence, reasoning clarity, correct L3 role boundary, escalation where needed, limitation language, and artifact completeness. Do not use AI to produce the final answer; use AI only as a coach for guided questions and clarity checks. Level-specific review correction: The final portfolio must show integration-readiness maturity: intake, evidence validation, handoff mapping, risk classification, closure, and transfer to timing/performance review.", "accepted_file_types": [".xlsx", ".docx", ".pdf", ".zip"], "submission_required": true, "expected_submission_type": "file"}], "templates": [{"id": "at-emb-fpga-l3-m6", "version": 1, "file_url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/templates/emb-fpga-l3/ART_L3_M6_Integration_Portfolio_Template", "file_name": "ART_L3_M6_Integration_Portfolio_Template", "file_type": "xlsx", "artifact_id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m6-f", "question_id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m6-f1", "is_downloadable": true}], "submissions": [], "total_score": 100, "max_attempts": 3, "artifact_type": "final", "passing_score": 70, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": true}, "express": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l3-m6-p", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l3-m6-p1", "order": 1, "title": "Practice Draft: L3 Integration Portfolio Pack", "description": "Can a reviewer trace your final decision through all prior artifacts and see a connected evidence-based capability proof for SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs?", "instructions": "Draft a first pass using the full module problem and required fields: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation.. Think through the evidence manually first and use AI only as a coach for questions, clarity checks, missed-risk prompts, or limitation checks. Level-specific review correction: The final portfolio must show integration-readiness maturity: intake, evidence validation, handoff mapping, risk classification, closure, and transfer to timing/performance review.", "submission_required": false, "expected_submission_type": "text"}], "templates": [], "submissions": [], "total_score": 0, "max_attempts": null, "artifact_type": "practice", "passing_score": null, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": false}}, "knowledge": {"technical_concepts_learnt": "SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs technical focus: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation. The final portfolio must show integration-readiness maturity: intake, evidence validation, handoff mapping, risk classification, closure, and transfer to timing/performance review.", "academic_refresher_concepts": "Portfolio structure, artifact continuity, evidence mapping, self-review, transfer reflection"}, "module_no": 6, "description": "Build the L3 Integration Portfolio Pack by completing this L3 Embedded Design Engineer task without truncating the case context: The reviewer must see that you can translate FPGA design evidence into integration-ready documentation. You must compile the complete L3 artifact chain into an integration portfolio. The final portfolio must show integration-readiness maturity: intake, evidence validation, handoff mapping, risk classification, closure, and transfer to timing/performance review.", "estimatedTime": "4 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may submit separate worksheets without proving a connected artifact chain, decision trail, and transfer readiness for the next level.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "artifact chain, evidence map, retry log, capability proof, transfer note, reviewer validation. Required workbook field focus: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation.", "reason_behind_concept": "Portfolio validation proves capability maturity by connecting every artifact into one evidence-backed decision trail. The final portfolio must show integration-readiness maturity: intake, evidence validation, handoff mapping, risk classification, closure, and transfer to timing/performance review.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L3 SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: L3 Integration Portfolio Pack. Module problem context: The reviewer must see that you can translate FPGA design evidence into integration-ready documentation. You must compile the complete L3 artifact chain into an integration portfolio. The final portfolio must show integration-readiness maturity: intake, evidence validation, handoff mapping, risk classification, closure, and transfer to timing/performance review."}, "student_confusion": "How do I prove that my final decision came from a connected evidence trail rather than isolated tasks?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L3 Portfolio, Transfer & Final Validation", "module_specific_problem_statement": "The reviewer must see that you can translate FPGA design evidence into integration-ready documentation. You must compile the complete L3 artifact chain into an integration portfolio. The final portfolio must show integration-readiness maturity: intake, evidence validation, handoff mapping, risk classification, closure, and transfer to timing/performance review."}], "duration": "18 hours", "course_id": "crs-emb-fpga-l3", "xp_reward": 950, "started_at": null, "why_needed": "Embedded design engineers need SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs because workplace review decisions depend on visible evidence, not assumptions or unverified implementation confidence. This L3 course is integration-readiness focused: you prepare a handoff using evidence from CPU access notes, bus mapping, memory interface notes, interrupt routing, and interface gaps, without redesigning SoC architecture.", "course_code": "EMB-FPGA-L3", "description": "You can prepare an SoC/FPGA integration-readiness handoff by validating IP blocks, bus mappings, CPU/MCU interaction, memory access, interrupts, and interface ownership. This L3 course is integration-readiness focused: you prepare a handoff using evidence from CPU access notes, bus mapping, memory interface notes, interrupt routing, and interface gaps, without redesigning SoC architecture.", "domain_name": "Embedded Systems / FPGA Design", "level_label": "L3", "sequence_no": 3, "completed_at": null, "how_to_build": "Learners build capability by completing seven connected artifacts using the full case evidence, required workbook fields, role boundaries, risk/escalation language, and human-first engineering judgement. This L3 course is integration-readiness focused: you prepare a handoff using evidence from CPU access notes, bus mapping, memory interface notes, interrupt routing, and interface gaps, without redesigning SoC architecture.", "current_level": 1, "priority_band": "A", "thumbnail_url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1518770660439-4636190af475?q=80&w=2670&auto=format&fit=crop", "required_level": 3, "capability_name": "FPGA SoC Integration Readiness", "difficulty_level": "intermediate", "job_pathway_role": "Embedded Design Engineer", "problem_artifacts": [{"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l3-m0", "no": 1, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L3 Integration Readiness Sheet", "module": "Industry, Role & Course Readiness", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Can you explain the L3 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case? Input: use the full L3 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: SoC integration evidence pack. Evidence given to you: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log. Evidence issues / actuals: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap. You decision required: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Action: complete the L3 Integration Readiness Sheet by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision. Evidence to cite: L3 integration-readiness purpose, CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, issue log, role boundary, and handoff expectation. Output decision: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you understands the work context before acting and can separate their own role from other engineering owners. Restriction: stay inside L3 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: Use platform terms carefully: CPU access note, bus mapping table, memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and integration issue log are the core evidence; examples such as ARM/NIOS, AXI/Avalon, or DDR/QDR depend on the actual platform. You prepare integration handoff evidence and escalation points; you do not redesign SoC bus architecture, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface mapping.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l3-m1", "no": 2, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L3 Integration Case Intake Note", "module": "Case Intake & Role Boundary", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Has you converted the senior engineer request into a scoped case intake by separating facts, assumptions, unknowns, role ownership, and clarification questions? Input: use the full L3 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: SoC integration evidence pack. Evidence given to you: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log. Evidence issues / actuals: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap. You decision required: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Action: complete the L3 Integration Case Intake Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Case request; available inputs; missing information; knowns; unknowns; assumptions; role boundary; clarification questions; intake decision. Evidence to cite: senior engineer request, available integration evidence, unknown interface contract, software/FPGA/memory/interrupt owner questions, assumptions, and unsafe gaps. Output decision: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you converted a vague workplace request into a scoped technical case with clear assumptions and questions. Restriction: stay inside L3 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: You clarifies what AI/embedded integration evidence is available and what must be confirmed by software, FPGA, memory-interface, or senior integration owners. You prepare integration handoff evidence and escalation points; you do not redesign SoC bus architecture, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface mapping.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l3-m2", "no": 3, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L3 SoC Evidence Validation Checklist", "module": "Evidence / Requirement Validation", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Has you validated the actual engineering evidence for SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs using the required fields: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision.? Input: use the full L3 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: SoC integration evidence pack. Evidence given to you: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log. Evidence issues / actuals: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap. You decision required: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Action: complete the L3 SoC Evidence Validation Checklist by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Evidence item; source/owner; completeness check; consistency check; mismatch/gap count; impact on design or validation; readiness decision. Evidence to cite: 4 IP blocks, bus-address conflicts, memory-timing warnings, interrupt owner gap, register/software-access mismatch, lane-mapping gap, and application or software-access alignment. Output decision: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you inspected actual evidence gaps and made a defensible proceed, retry, or block decision. Restriction: stay inside L3 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: Make software-access alignment visible: you checks whether register fields, address map, CPU access note, and software-facing documentation agree before integration planning continues. You prepare integration handoff evidence and escalation points; you do not redesign SoC bus architecture, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface mapping.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l3-m3", "no": 4, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L3 Interface Handoff Note", "module": "Guided Work Action or Handoff", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Is your action or handoff note based on visible engineering evidence, documented reasoning, and a safe L3 boundary rather than unsupported fixing or overclaiming? Input: use the full L3 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: SoC integration evidence pack. Evidence given to you: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log. Evidence issues / actuals: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap. You decision required: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Action: complete the L3 Interface Handoff Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Evidence reviewed; action performed; issue counts or observations; human-first reasoning; engineering impact; handoff message; final action decision. Evidence to cite: selected inputs, current register/address map, CPU/software access agreement, proposed handoff fields, missing interface or owner fields, and integration-readable handoff message. Output decision: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you performed a concrete engineering action and made the output usable by the next stakeholder. Restriction: stay inside L3 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: You map bus conflicts, register-field mismatches, interrupt ownership gaps, memory-interface warnings, and lane-mapping gaps into an integration handoff note. You do not redesign the SoC bus, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface. You prepare integration handoff evidence and escalation points; you do not redesign SoC bus architecture, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface mapping.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l3-m4", "no": 5, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note", "module": "Exceptions, Blockers, Risks & Escalations", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Has you identified blockers, risks, ownership gaps, severity, escalation reason, and next action using module evidence? Input: use the full L3 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: SoC integration evidence pack. Evidence given to you: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log. Evidence issues / actuals: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap. You decision required: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Action: complete the L3 Integration Risk and Escalation Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Risk ID; risk category; observed evidence; consequence; severity; owner; escalation reason; recommended next action. Evidence to cite: bus conflicts, memory timing warnings, interrupt ownership gap, register/software-access mismatch, lane-mapping gap, severity, owner, and escalation reason. Output decision: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you links evidence to consequence, severity, owner, and escalation logic. Restriction: stay inside L3 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: High-speed lane mapping gaps are escalation risks requiring senior or interface-owner confirmation. Memory timing warnings and interrupt ownership gaps should be classified as handoff or blocker items, not you-owned fixes. You prepare integration handoff evidence and escalation points; you do not redesign SoC bus architecture, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface mapping.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l3-m5", "no": 6, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report", "module": "Quality, Communication & Closure", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Is the final closure message clear about what was checked, what remains limited, what decision is defensible, and what the next team should do? Input: use the full L3 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: SoC integration evidence pack. Evidence given to you: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log. Evidence issues / actuals: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap. You decision required: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Action: complete the L3 Integration Quality and Closure Report by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Work completed; evidence used; checks passed; checks failed; open issues; closure message; confidence level; closure decision. Evidence to cite: work completed, evidence used, interface gaps, latency or timing assumptions where applicable, owner actions, closure confidence, and integration-readiness decision. Output decision: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you communicate a clear, honest closure decision without overclaiming readiness. Restriction: stay inside L3 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: The closure must state whether the evidence is usable for integration planning, partially ready with interface changes, or blocked until software-access, memory, interrupt, or lane-mapping gaps are resolved. You prepare integration handoff evidence and escalation points; you do not redesign SoC bus architecture, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface mapping.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l3-m6", "no": 7, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L3 Integration Portfolio Pack", "module": "Portfolio, Transfer & Final Validation", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Can a reviewer trace your final decision through all prior artifacts and see a connected evidence-based capability proof for SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs? Input: use the full L3 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: SoC integration evidence pack. Evidence given to you: 4 IP blocks; ARM/NIOS CPU access note; AXI/Avalon bus table; DDR/QDR memory summary; interrupt routing notes; integration issue log. Evidence issues / actuals: 2 bus-address conflicts; 3 memory-timing warnings; 1 interrupt owner gap; 2 register/software-access mismatches; 1 lane mapping gap. You decision required: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Action: complete the L3 Integration Portfolio Pack by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation. Evidence to cite: complete L3 artifact chain and evidence trail leading toward L4 timing/performance review. Output decision: Ready for integration planning / partially ready / blocked. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you connects all artifacts into a coherent capability proof. Restriction: stay inside L3 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: The final portfolio must show integration-readiness maturity: intake, evidence validation, handoff mapping, risk classification, closure, and transfer to timing/performance review. You prepare integration handoff evidence and escalation points; you do not redesign SoC bus architecture, memory controller, interrupt architecture, or high-speed interface mapping.", "status": "not_started"}], "problem_statement": {"title": "FPGA SoC Integration Readiness with Embedded CPUs and Bus Interfaces", "description": "After the L2 simulation review, the senior engineer wants to know whether the design can move toward FPGA SoC integration planning. You receive an integration map with 4 IP blocks, an ARM/NIOS CPU access note, an AXI/Avalon bus mapping table, a DDR/QDR memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and an integration issue log. The evidence shows 2 bus-address conflicts, 3 memory-timing warnings, 1 interrupt line without a confirmed owner, 2 register fields not aligned with the software-access note, and 1 high-speed interface lane mapping gap. You must decide whether the design is ready for integration planning, partially ready with interface corrections, or blocked until bus, memory, interrupt, and ownership gaps are resolved. This L3 course is integration-readiness focused: you prepare a handoff using evidence from CPU access notes, bus mapping, memory interface notes, interrupt routing, and interface gaps, without redesigning SoC architecture."}, "certification_value": "Portfolio-ready L3 evidence for SoC/FPGA integration readiness across CPU, bus, memory, interrupt, and interface handoffs.", "completion_percentage": 0, "common_course_problem_statement": "After the L2 simulation review, the senior engineer wants to know whether the design can move toward FPGA SoC integration planning. You receive an integration map with 4 IP blocks, an ARM/NIOS CPU access note, an AXI/Avalon bus mapping table, a DDR/QDR memory interface summary, interrupt routing notes, and an integration issue log. The evidence shows 2 bus-address conflicts, 3 memory-timing warnings, 1 interrupt line without a confirmed owner, 2 register fields not aligned with the software-access note, and 1 high-speed interface lane mapping gap. You must decide whether the design is ready for integration planning, partially ready with interface corrections, or blocked until bus, memory, interrupt, and ownership gaps are resolved. This L3 course is integration-readiness focused: you prepare a handoff using evidence from CPU access notes, bus mapping, memory interface notes, interrupt routing, and interface gaps, without redesigning SoC architecture."}, {"id": "lpc-emb-fpga-l4", "gap": 2, "badge": "Timing and Performance Control", "title": "Timing Closure, Performance Validation, and Interface Reliability for FPGA Systems", "status": "not_started", "modules": [{"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l4-m0", "title": "Industry, Role & Course Readiness", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L4_M0_Timing_Readiness_Template; you evidence inputs: Post-synthesis timing report; utilization summary; CDC warnings; throughput note; performance validation log; workbook field focus: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision.", "tool_type_free_or_paid": "template + evidence pack"}, "stages": [{"key": "engage", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explore", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explain", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "express", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "empower", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "evolve", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}], "status": "available", "content": [{"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-empower", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-empower", "order": 5, "stage": "empower", "title": "Decision check for L4 Timing Readiness Sheet: defend L4 readiness using visible evidence and limitations", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Readiness Sheet: What decision can you defend from the evidence at this stage?", "follow_up": "Make the decision yourself using evidence: timing-confidence blocked, retest or optimization required, or escalation required before performance confidence; do not ask AI to decide for you."}], "content_type": "checklist", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-engage", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-engage", "order": 1, "stage": "engage", "title": "L4 L4 Timing Readiness Sheet kickoff: workplace risk, role boundary, and why timing-closure evidence review, performance validation, utilization, CDC, and interface reliability control cannot be assumed", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Readiness Sheet: What workplace risk appears in this module before you propose any action?", "follow_up": "Name the workplace risk in M0 using timing-closure evidence review, not you-performed timing closure or implementation sign-off language before suggesting any action."}], "content_type": "case brief", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-evolve", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-evolve", "order": 6, "stage": "evolve", "title": "Reflection for L4 Timing Readiness Sheet: transfer this reasoning to the next embedded FPGA level or workplace case", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Readiness Sheet: How does this module prepare you for the next module, next level, or another embedded FPGA case?", "follow_up": "State one reusable rule for moving from this level toward L5 end-to-end validation without overclaiming responsibility."}], "content_type": "reflection prompt", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-explain", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-explain", "order": 3, "stage": "explain", "title": "Guide for L4 Timing Readiness Sheet: concepts, ownership boundary, and safe you decision language", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Readiness Sheet: Which concept or boundary explains why this evidence matters?", "follow_up": "Explain the key concept in simple engineering language and connect it to timing-closure evidence review, not you-performed timing closure or implementation sign-off."}], "content_type": "explainer", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-explore", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-explore", "order": 2, "stage": "explore", "title": "Inspect evidence for L4 Timing Readiness Sheet: WNS -0.42 ns; 23 violated timing paths; 2 unresolved CDC warnings; 78% logic utilization; 64% block memory; 5.8 Gbps measured vs 6.25 Gbps target", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Readiness Sheet: What exact evidence should you inspect before filling this artifact?", "follow_up": "Point to the exact evidence for M0: post-synthesis timing report, WNS -0.42 ns, 23 violated paths, 2 unresolved CDC warnings, 78% logic utilization, 64% block memory utilization, and 5.8 Gbps vs 6.25 Gbps throughput evidence."}], "content_type": "practice activity", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-express", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m0-express", "order": 4, "stage": "express", "title": "Template task: complete the L4 Timing Readiness Sheet using the full module problem and required workbook fields", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Readiness Sheet: What will you write in the artifact so a reviewer can verify your work?", "follow_up": "Write the artifact using M0 evidence; include facts, assumptions, owner boundary, and decision evidence."}], "content_type": "worksheet", "duration_seconds": 1200}], "support": {"ai_support_tips": "Ask AI to explain the L4 boundary in simple terms, then rewrite the role responsibility in your own words before filling the readiness sheet. Frame the work as timing-closure evidence review and timing-readiness review. You can group violations, identify blockers, and prepare an optimization handoff, but should not be expected to close timing.", "module_continuity_link": "Creates the readiness context that feeds Module 1 case intake. Frame the work as timing-closure evidence review and timing-readiness review. You can group violations, identify blockers, and prepare an optimization handoff, but should not be expected to close timing."}, "artifacts": {"empower": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l4-m0-f", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l4-m0-f1", "order": 1, "title": "L4 Timing Readiness Sheet", "description": "Can you explain the L4 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case? You review timing-closure evidence and performance risk; they do not perform timing closure or claim implementation sign-off.", "instructions": "Complete the final artifact by answering the workplace artifact question: Can you explain the L4 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case? Fill the required workbook fields fully: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision. Reviewer expectation: Checks whether you understands the work context before acting and can separate their own role from other engineering owners. Submit with visible evidence, reasoning clarity, correct L4 role boundary, escalation where needed, limitation language, and artifact completeness. Do not use AI to produce the final answer; use AI only as a coach for guided questions and clarity checks. Level-specific review correction: Frame the work as timing-closure evidence review and timing-readiness review. You can group violations, identify blockers, and prepare an optimization handoff, but should not be expected to close timing.", "accepted_file_types": [".xlsx", ".docx", ".pdf", ".zip"], "submission_required": true, "expected_submission_type": "file"}], "templates": [{"id": "at-emb-fpga-l4-m0", "version": 1, "file_url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/templates/emb-fpga-l4/ART_L4_M0_Timing_Readiness_Template", "file_name": "ART_L4_M0_Timing_Readiness_Template", "file_type": "xlsx", "artifact_id": "ma-emb-fpga-l4-m0-f", "question_id": "aq-emb-fpga-l4-m0-f1", "is_downloadable": true}], "submissions": [], "total_score": 100, "max_attempts": 3, "artifact_type": "final", "passing_score": 70, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": true}, "express": {"id": "ma-emb-fpga-l4-m0-p", "questions": [{"id": "aq-emb-fpga-l4-m0-p1", "order": 1, "title": "Practice Draft: L4 Timing Readiness Sheet", "description": "Can you explain the L4 embedded FPGA work context, role boundary, evidence pack, and readiness decision before acting on the case? You review timing-closure evidence and performance risk; they do not perform timing closure or claim implementation sign-off.", "instructions": "Draft a first pass using the full module problem and required fields: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision.. Think through the evidence manually first and use AI only as a coach for questions, clarity checks, missed-risk prompts, or limitation checks. Level-specific review correction: Frame the work as timing-closure evidence review and timing-readiness review. You can group violations, identify blockers, and prepare an optimization handoff, but should not be expected to close timing.", "submission_required": false, "expected_submission_type": "text"}], "templates": [], "submissions": [], "total_score": 0, "max_attempts": null, "artifact_type": "practice", "passing_score": null, "allow_resubmission": true, "require_review_unlock": false}}, "knowledge": {"technical_concepts_learnt": "timing-closure evidence review, performance validation, utilization, CDC, and interface reliability control technical focus: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision. Frame the work as timing-closure evidence review and timing-readiness review. You can group violations, identify blockers, and prepare an optimization handoff, but should not be expected to close timing.", "academic_refresher_concepts": "Embedded systems workflow, FPGA/HDL review basics, role responsibility, evidence readiness"}, "module_no": 0, "description": "Build the L4 Timing Readiness Sheet by completing this L4 Embedded Design Engineer task without truncating the case context: You are moving from integration readiness to timing-closure evidence review and performance validation. The risk is that the design may appear integrated but fail timing, CDC, utilization, or throughput targets. You must understand timing reports, violated paths, CDC warnings, utilization pressure, and interface throughput before making a performance confidence claim. Frame the work as timing-closure evidence review and timing-readiness review. You can group violations, identify blockers, and prepare an optimization handoff, but should not be expected to close timing.", "estimatedTime": "2 hrs", "pressure_points": "You may start technical work without understanding why timing closure, performance validation, utilization, CDC, and interface reliability control depends on evidence readiness, role scope, and ownership boundaries.", "learning_content": {"major_concepts": "work context, role boundary, evidence readiness, non-owned dependencies, readiness decision. Required workbook field focus: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision.", "reason_behind_concept": "Readiness understanding prevents you from treating a limited evidence pack as a complete engineering approval. Frame the work as timing-closure evidence review and timing-readiness review. You can group violations, identify blockers, and prepare an optimization handoff, but should not be expected to close timing.", "implementation_context_when_to_use": "Use this module inside the L4 timing-closure evidence review, performance validation, utilization, CDC, and interface reliability control course when you must turn the supplied case evidence into the artifact: L4 Timing Readiness Sheet. Module problem context: You are moving from integration readiness to timing-closure evidence review and performance validation. The risk is that the design may appear integrated but fail timing, CDC, utilization, or throughput targets. You must understand timing reports, violated paths, CDC warnings, utilization pressure, and interface throughput before making a performance confidence claim. Frame the work as timing-closure evidence review and timing-readiness review. You can group violations, identify blockers, and prepare an optimization handoff, but should not be expected to close timing."}, "student_confusion": "What does L4 mean here, and am I supposed to solve the full embedded design problem or validate readiness evidence?", "artifact_submitted": false, "completion_percentage": 0, "engineering_workflow_stage": "L4 Industry, Role & Course Readiness", "module_specific_problem_statement": "You are moving from integration readiness to timing-closure evidence review and performance validation. The risk is that the design may appear integrated but fail timing, CDC, utilization, or throughput targets. You must understand timing reports, violated paths, CDC warnings, utilization pressure, and interface throughput before making a performance confidence claim. Frame the work as timing-closure evidence review and timing-readiness review. You can group violations, identify blockers, and prepare an optimization handoff, but should not be expected to close timing."}, {"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l4-m1", "title": "Case Intake & Role Boundary", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L4_M1_Timing_Case_Intake_Template; you evidence inputs: Post-synthesis timing report; utilization summary; CDC warnings; throughput note; performance validation log; workbook field focus: Case request; available inputs; missing information; knowns; unknowns; assumptions; role boundary; clarification questions; intake decision.", "tool_type_free_or_paid": "template + evidence pack"}, "stages": [{"key": "engage", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explore", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explain", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "express", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "empower", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "evolve", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}], "status": "available", "content": [{"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-empower", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-empower", "order": 5, "stage": "empower", "title": "Decision check for L4 Timing Case Intake Note: defend L4 readiness using visible evidence and limitations", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Case Intake Note: What decision can you defend from the evidence at this stage?", "follow_up": "Make the decision yourself using evidence: timing-confidence blocked, retest or optimization required, or escalation required before performance confidence; do not ask AI to decide for you."}], "content_type": "checklist", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-engage", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-engage", "order": 1, "stage": "engage", "title": "L4 L4 Timing Case Intake Note kickoff: workplace risk, role boundary, and why timing-closure evidence review, performance validation, utilization, CDC, and interface reliability control cannot be assumed", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Case Intake Note: What workplace risk appears in this module before you propose any action?", "follow_up": "Name the workplace risk in M1 using timing-closure evidence review, not you-performed timing closure or implementation sign-off language before suggesting any action."}], "content_type": "case brief", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-evolve", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-evolve", "order": 6, "stage": "evolve", "title": "Reflection for L4 Timing Case Intake Note: transfer this reasoning to the next embedded FPGA level or workplace case", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Case Intake Note: How does this module prepare you for the next module, next level, or another embedded FPGA case?", "follow_up": "State one reusable rule for moving from this level toward L5 end-to-end validation without overclaiming responsibility."}], "content_type": "reflection prompt", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-explain", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-explain", "order": 3, "stage": "explain", "title": "Guide for L4 Timing Case Intake Note: concepts, ownership boundary, and safe you decision language", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Case Intake Note: Which concept or boundary explains why this evidence matters?", "follow_up": "Explain the key concept in simple engineering language and connect it to timing-closure evidence review, not you-performed timing closure or implementation sign-off."}], "content_type": "explainer", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-explore", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l4/content/c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-explore", "order": 2, "stage": "explore", "title": "Inspect evidence for L4 Timing Case Intake Note: WNS -0.42 ns; 23 violated timing paths; 2 unresolved CDC warnings; 78% logic utilization; 64% block memory; 5.8 Gbps measured vs 6.25 Gbps target", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L4 Timing Case Intake Note: What exact evidence should you inspect before filling this artifact?", "follow_up": "Point to the exact evidence for M1: post-synthesis timing report, WNS -0.42 ns, 23 violated paths, 2 unresolved CDC warnings, 78% logic utilization, 64% block memory utilization, and 5.8 Gbps vs 6.25 Gbps throughput evidence."}], "content_type": "practice activity", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l4-m1-express", "url": "https://cont