ntil timing closure evidence or targeted optimization evidence is provided. You should also note the 5.8 Gbps vs 6.25 Gbps throughput gap as an approximately 7.2% shortfall. You performs timing-closure evidence review, not timing closure. Negative slack and violated paths block timing-confidence claims until timing closure or targeted optimization evidence is provided. Unresolved CDC warnings require owner confirmation, waiver justification, synchronization evidence, or blocker status. Throughput shortfall is about 7.2%. Utilization is a pressure signal unless linked to timing, routing, or expansion risk.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l4-m3", "no": 4, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L4 Performance Optimization Handoff Note", "module": "Guided Work Action or Handoff", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Is your action or handoff note based on visible engineering evidence, documented reasoning, and a safe L4 boundary rather than unsupported fixing or overclaiming? Input: use the full L4 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: Timing and performance evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Post-synthesis timing report; utilization summary; CDC warnings; throughput note; performance validation log. Evidence issues / actuals: WNS -0.42 ns; 23 violated timing paths; 2 unresolved CDC warnings; 78% logic utilization; 64% block memory; 5.8 Gbps measured vs 6.25 Gbps target. You decision required: Continue optimization / retest / blocked for timing closure. Action: complete the L4 Performance Optimization Handoff Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Evidence reviewed; action performed; issue counts or observations; human-first reasoning; engineering impact; handoff message; final action decision. Evidence to cite: grouped violated paths, negative slack evidence, CDC warning status, throughput shortfall, utilization pressure, missing place-and-route evidence, and owner handoff. Output decision: Continue optimization / retest / blocked for timing closure. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you performed a concrete engineering action and made the output usable by the next stakeholder. Restriction: stay inside L4 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: You review timing and performance evidence, groups violated paths, records WNS/TNS-style evidence where available, notes the throughput shortfall, and prepares a handoff. You do not perform final timing closure or claim implementation sign-off. You performs timing-closure evidence review, not timing closure. Negative slack and violated paths block timing-confidence claims until timing closure or targeted optimization evidence is provided. Unresolved CDC warnings require owner confirmation, waiver justification, synchronization evidence, or blocker status. Throughput shortfall is about 7.2%. Utilization is a pressure signal unless linked to timing, routing, or expansion risk.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l4-m4", "no": 5, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L4 Timing Risk and Escalation Note", "module": "Exceptions, Blockers, Risks & Escalations", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Has you identified blockers, risks, ownership gaps, severity, escalation reason, and next action using module evidence? Input: use the full L4 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: Timing and performance evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Post-synthesis timing report; utilization summary; CDC warnings; throughput note; performance validation log. Evidence issues / actuals: WNS -0.42 ns; 23 violated timing paths; 2 unresolved CDC warnings; 78% logic utilization; 64% block memory; 5.8 Gbps measured vs 6.25 Gbps target. You decision required: Continue optimization / retest / blocked for timing closure. Action: complete the L4 Timing Risk and Escalation Note by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Risk ID; risk category; observed evidence; consequence; severity; owner; escalation reason; recommended next action. Evidence to cite: negative slack, violated paths, unresolved CDC warnings, throughput shortfall of about 7.2%, utilization pressure, missing waiver/synchronization evidence, severity, owner, and escalation reason. Output decision: Continue optimization / retest / blocked for timing closure. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you links evidence to consequence, severity, owner, and escalation logic. Restriction: stay inside L4 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: Unresolved CDC warnings are escalation-level risks unless the clock-domain owner provides justification, waiver, or synchronization evidence. Utilization is a pressure signal, not a blocker by itself, unless linked to timing failure, routing congestion, or future expansion risk. You performs timing-closure evidence review, not timing closure. Negative slack and violated paths block timing-confidence claims until timing closure or targeted optimization evidence is provided. Unresolved CDC warnings require owner confirmation, waiver justification, synchronization evidence, or blocker status. Throughput shortfall is about 7.2%. Utilization is a pressure signal unless linked to timing, routing, or expansion risk.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l4-m5", "no": 6, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L4 Performance Quality and Closure Report", "module": "Quality, Communication & Closure", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Is the final closure message clear about what was checked, what remains limited, what decision is defensible, and what the next team should do? Input: use the full L4 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: Timing and performance evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Post-synthesis timing report; utilization summary; CDC warnings; throughput note; performance validation log. Evidence issues / actuals: WNS -0.42 ns; 23 violated timing paths; 2 unresolved CDC warnings; 78% logic utilization; 64% block memory; 5.8 Gbps measured vs 6.25 Gbps target. You decision required: Continue optimization / retest / blocked for timing closure. Action: complete the L4 Performance Quality and Closure Report by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Work completed; evidence used; checks passed; checks failed; open issues; closure message; confidence level; closure decision. Evidence to cite: timing/performance checks completed, blockers, retest or optimization needs, place-and-route evidence gaps, owner actions, limitation language, and closure decision. Output decision: Continue optimization / retest / blocked for timing closure. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you communicate a clear, honest closure decision without overclaiming readiness. Restriction: stay inside L4 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: The closure must clearly state that WNS -0.42 ns, 23 violated paths, unresolved CDC warnings, and 5.8 Gbps vs 6.25 Gbps performance prevent performance-confidence claims unless retest, optimization, owner waiver, or place-and-route evidence is supplied. You performs timing-closure evidence review, not timing closure. Negative slack and violated paths block timing-confidence claims until timing closure or targeted optimization evidence is provided. Unresolved CDC warnings require owner confirmation, waiver justification, synchronization evidence, or blocker status. Throughput shortfall is about 7.2%. Utilization is a pressure signal unless linked to timing, routing, or expansion risk.", "status": "not_started"}, {"id": "pa-emb-fpga-l4-m6", "no": 7, "text": "", "score": 0, "title": "L4 Timing Portfolio Pack", "module": "Portfolio, Transfer & Final Validation", "prompt": "Final artifact question: Can a reviewer trace your final decision through all prior artifacts and see a connected evidence-based capability proof for timing closure, performance validation, utilization, CDC, and interface reliability control? Input: use the full L4 course problem statement, the module-specific problem statement, prior module artifacts where applicable, and this level evidence: Evidence pack: Timing and performance evidence pack. Evidence given to you: Post-synthesis timing report; utilization summary; CDC warnings; throughput note; performance validation log. Evidence issues / actuals: WNS -0.42 ns; 23 violated timing paths; 2 unresolved CDC warnings; 78% logic utilization; 64% block memory; 5.8 Gbps measured vs 6.25 Gbps target. You decision required: Continue optimization / retest / blocked for timing closure. Action: complete the L4 Timing Portfolio Pack by filling these required workbook fields without shortening the text: Portfolio cover note; artifact chain; evidence map; retry log; capability proof; transfer note; reviewer validation. Evidence to cite: complete timing/performance risk-review artifact chain and transfer evidence for L5 end-to-end validation. Output decision: Continue optimization / retest / blocked for timing closure. Reviewer reads this artifact as follows: Checks whether you connects all artifacts into a coherent capability proof. Restriction: stay inside L4 Embedded Design Engineer responsibility. Do not claim full production approval, diagnose unproven root cause, or take ownership of software/test/integration/timing decisions that require senior or cross-team confirmation. Use AI only as a coach for guided questions, not as the final answer provider. Level-specific correction: The final portfolio must prove timing/performance risk-review capability, not timing-closure execution. It should transfer unresolved evidence to L5 end-to-end validation with clear blockers and owner actions. You performs timing-closure evidence review, not timing closure. Negative slack and violated paths block timing-confidence claims until timing closure or targeted optimization evidence is provided. Unresolved CDC warnings require owner confirmation, waiver justification, synchronization evidence, or blocker status. Throughput shortfall is about 7.2%. Utilization is a pressure signal unless linked to timing, routing, or expansion risk.", "status": "not_started"}], "problem_statement": {"title": "Timing Closure, Performance Validation, and Interface Reliability for FPGA Systems", "description": "After L3 integration-readiness review, the FPGA design is synthesized for a controlled performance check. You receive a post-synthesis timing report, utilization summary, CDC warning list, interface throughput note, and performance validation log. The timing report shows worst negative slack of -0.42 ns, 23 violated timing paths, 2 unresolved clock-domain crossing warnings, 78 percent logic utilization, 64 percent block memory utilization, and an interface throughput result of 5.8 Gbps against an expected 6.25 Gbps target. You must decide whether the design can continue with optimization, must be blocked for timing closure, or needs targeted retesting before performance confidence can be claimed. This L4 course is a timing-closure evidence review and performance-risk review, not a course where you review timing-closure evidence. Negative slack, violated paths, unresolved CDC warnings, and throughput shortfall must be treated as evidence requiring review, retest, optimization handoff, or escalation."}, "certification_value": "Portfolio-ready L4 evidence for timing closure, performance validation, utilization, CDC, and interface reliability control.", "completion_percentage": 0, "common_course_problem_statement": "After L3 integration-readiness review, the FPGA design is synthesized for a controlled performance check. You receive a post-synthesis timing report, utilization summary, CDC warning list, interface throughput note, and performance validation log. The timing report shows worst negative slack of -0.42 ns, 23 violated timing paths, 2 unresolved clock-domain crossing warnings, 78 percent logic utilization, 64 percent block memory utilization, and an interface throughput result of 5.8 Gbps against an expected 6.25 Gbps target. You must decide whether the design can continue with optimization, must be blocked for timing closure, or needs targeted retesting before performance confidence can be claimed. This L4 course is a timing-closure evidence review and performance-risk review, not a course where you review timing-closure evidence. Negative slack, violated paths, unresolved CDC warnings, and throughput shortfall must be treated as evidence requiring review, retest, optimization handoff, or escalation."}, {"id": "lpc-emb-fpga-l5", "gap": 2, "badge": "Embedded FPGA Solution Validation", "title": "End-to-End Embedded FPGA Design Validation and Certification Portfolio", "status": "not_started", "modules": [{"id": "mod-emb-fpga-l5-m0", "title": "Industry, Role & Course Readiness", "tools": {"tools_used": "ART_L5_M0_Solution_Readiness_Template; you evidence inputs: L1-L4 evidence chain; interface docs; simulation corrections; integration map; timing reports; throughput validation; 11-risk register; workbook field focus: Level purpose; system/workflow context; evidence pack components; you responsibility; non-owned dependencies; readiness gaps; readiness decision.", "tool_type_free_or_paid": "template + evidence pack"}, "stages": [{"key": "engage", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explore", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "explain", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "express", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "empower", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}, {"key": "evolve", "status": "not_started", "time_spent_seconds": 0}], "status": "available", "content": [{"id": "c-emb-fpga-l5-m0-empower", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l5/content/c-emb-fpga-l5-m0-empower", "order": 5, "stage": "empower", "title": "Decision check for L5 Solution Readiness Sheet: defend L5 readiness using visible evidence and limitations", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L5 Solution Readiness Sheet: What decision can you defend from the evidence at this stage?", "follow_up": "Make the decision yourself using evidence: supervised prototype validation only, needs controlled testing, or blocks release/production use; do not ask AI to decide for you."}], "content_type": "checklist", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l5-m0-engage", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l5/content/c-emb-fpga-l5-m0-engage", "order": 1, "stage": "engage", "title": "L5 L5 Solution Readiness Sheet kickoff: workplace risk, role boundary, and why end-to-end embedded FPGA decision-support validation and certification-level portfolio proof cannot be assumed", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L5 Solution Readiness Sheet: What workplace risk appears in this module before you propose any action?", "follow_up": "Name the workplace risk in M0 using supervised prototype validation only, not production release or autonomous system approval language before suggesting any action."}], "content_type": "case brief", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l5-m0-evolve", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l5/content/c-emb-fpga-l5-m0-evolve", "order": 6, "stage": "evolve", "title": "Reflection for L5 Solution Readiness Sheet: transfer this reasoning to the next embedded FPGA level or workplace case", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L5 Solution Readiness Sheet: How does this module prepare you for the next module, next level, or another embedded FPGA case?", "follow_up": "State one reusable rule for moving from this level toward workplace transfer and certification proof without overclaiming responsibility."}], "content_type": "reflection prompt", "duration_seconds": 1200}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l5-m0-explain", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l5/content/c-emb-fpga-l5-m0-explain", "order": 3, "stage": "explain", "title": "Guide for L5 Solution Readiness Sheet: concepts, ownership boundary, and safe you decision language", "status": "published", "isViewed": false, "facilitator": [{"question": "AI coach for L5 Solution Readiness Sheet: Which concept or boundary explains why this evidence matters?", "follow_up": "Explain the key concept in simple engineering language and connect it to supervised prototype validation only, not production release or autonomous system approval."}], "content_type": "explainer", "duration_seconds": 900}, {"id": "c-emb-fpga-l5-m0-explore", "url": "https://content.rareminds.dev/courses/emb-fpga-l5/content/c-emb-fpga-l5-m0-explore", "order": 2, "stage": "explore", "title": "Inspect evidence for L5 Solution Readiness She